window.GIQ={"meta":{"tier":1,"lede":"","top":74,"fac":{"n":74,"nsrc":6}},"lectures":[{"id":"HIST-11","t":"Digestive tract (H15A, H15B)"},{"id":"HIST-12","t":"Glands of the digestive system (H16)"}],"groups":[{"l":"HIST-11","u":0,"label":"Filiform papillae: pointed, keratinized, no taste buds","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G018-17.jpg","cap":"Four papilla types: filiform panel pointed and keratinized, without taste buds","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17","lg":[["(c) arrows","deep clefts between the foliate ridges"],["(b) arrows","secondary connective tissue papillae pushing up into the epithelium"]]},{"src":"../sl/G015-35.jpg","cap":"Tongue surface: pointed filiform (FI) beside mushroom-shaped fungiform (F) papillae","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 35","lg":[["a","dorsal tongue surface with filiform and fungiform papillae"],["F","fungiform papilla (mushroom-shaped, lightly keratinized)"],["FI","filiform papillae (pointed, heavily keratinized)"],["SS","stratified squamous epithelium"],["CT","connective tissue core"],["b","one large vallate papilla"],["TB","taste buds on the papilla's side walls"],["GL","small serous (von Ebner) glands draining into the trench"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G018-49-1","s":"Q2. Name the type of papillae:","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Foliate papillae","C. Fungiform papillae","D. Circumvallate papillae"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Filiform papillae","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Filiform papillae.</b> Rows of slender, pointed cones capped with keratin (the pale layer at the tips). They have no taste buds. They give the tongue its rough surface and are the most numerous papillae.<br>B. Foliate: parallel ridges separated by deep clefts, with taste buds on the walls.<br>C. Fungiform: mushroom-shaped, with a connective-tissue core and taste buds on top.<br>D. Circumvallate: one large papilla in a circular trench, with taste buds on its walls.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-49.jpg","in":"Row of pointed, slanted papillae capped with keratin; no taste buds. Label blacked out.","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":49,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"n":"1"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":0,"label":"Circumvallate papilla: trench, taste buds, glands","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-35.jpg","cap":"Vallate papilla: trench, taste buds (TB) on walls, glands (GL) below","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 35","lg":[["a","dorsal tongue surface with filiform and fungiform papillae"],["F","fungiform papilla (mushroom-shaped, lightly keratinized)"],["FI","filiform papillae (pointed, heavily keratinized)"],["SS","stratified squamous epithelium"],["CT","connective tissue core"],["b","one large vallate papilla"],["TB","taste buds on the papilla's side walls"],["GL","small serous (von Ebner) glands draining into the trench"]]},{"src":"../sl/G018-17.jpg","cap":"Circumvallate papilla with taste buds on its walls and serous glands beneath","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17","lg":[["(c) arrows","deep clefts between the foliate ridges"],["(b) arrows","secondary connective tissue papillae pushing up into the epithelium"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G018-48-1","s":"Q1. Name the type of papillae:","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Foliate papillae","C. Fungiform papillae","D. Circumvallate papillae"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Circumvallate papillae","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Circumvallate papillae.</b> One large, flat-topped papilla sunk below the surface in a circular trench. Taste buds line its side walls, and serous (von Ebner) glands below it empty into the trench. These papillae form the V-shaped row just in front of the sulcus terminalis.<br>A. Filiform: many small, pointed, keratinized cones with no taste buds.<br>B. Foliate: several parallel ridges with clefts between them, on the sides of the tongue.<br>C. Fungiform: a single mushroom-shaped papilla raised above the surface, with taste buds on its top.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-48.jpg","in":"One large flat-topped papilla in a deep trench; taste buds labeled on its walls; serous glands labeled below. The name of the papilla is blacked out.","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":48,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"n":"2"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":0,"label":"Foliate papillae: parallel ridges with taste-bud clefts","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G018-17.jpg","cap":"Foliate papillae: parallel ridges, taste buds in clefts, serous glands below","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17","lg":[["(c) arrows","deep clefts between the foliate ridges"],["(b) arrows","secondary connective tissue papillae pushing up into the epithelium"]]},{"src":"../sl/G015-33.jpg","cap":"Diagrams of filiform, fungiform, vallate and foliate papillae","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"G018-50-1","s":"Q3. Name the type of papillae:","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Foliate papillae","C. Fungiform papillae","D. Circumvallate papillae"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Foliate papillae","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Foliate papillae.</b> Several parallel, leaf-like ridges separated by deep, narrow clefts. Taste buds line the walls of the clefts, and ducts of serous glands open into the clefts. They sit on the sides of the back of the tongue.<br>A. Filiform: pointed keratinized cones, no taste buds.<br>C. Fungiform: a single mushroom shape, not a row of ridges with clefts.<br>D. Circumvallate: one large papilla surrounded by a circular trench, not several parallel ridges.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-50.jpg","in":"Several parallel ridges separated by narrow clefts (arrows); taste buds, ducts and serous glands labeled. Label blacked out.","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":50,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["arrows","deep clefts between the foliate ridges"],["lines at bottom (label cut off)","striated (skeletal) muscle"]],"n":"3"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":1,"label":"Taste buds line vallate papilla walls","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-37.jpg","cap":"Taste buds: pale oval cell clusters within the papilla epithelium","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 37","lg":[["arrow","taste pore with the taste cells' microvilli"]]},{"src":"../sl/G015-35.jpg","cap":"Vallate papilla walls lined by taste buds (TB) facing the trench","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 35","lg":[["a","dorsal tongue surface with filiform and fungiform papillae"],["F","fungiform papilla (mushroom-shaped, lightly keratinized)"],["FI","filiform papillae (pointed, heavily keratinized)"],["SS","stratified squamous epithelium"],["CT","connective tissue core"],["b","one large vallate papilla"],["TB","taste buds on the papilla's side walls"],["GL","small serous (von Ebner) glands draining into the trench"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G118-18-1","s":"The structures labeled B in the image below are present in which region of the GI tract?","o":["A. Tongue","B. Small Intestines","C. Appendix","D. Lips"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Tongue","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Tongue.</b> The arrows labeled B point to <b>taste buds</b>: pale, oval, barrel-shaped groups of cells set inside a stratified squamous epithelium, on a wall that faces a deep cleft (C). That is the wall of a tongue papilla, where most taste buds sit.<br>B. Small intestines: a single layer of columnar cells with goblet cells, on villi; no taste buds.<br>C. Appendix: crypts lined by columnar cells and ringed by lymphoid follicles; no taste buds.<br>D. Lips: stratified squamous epithelium, but no taste buds; the skin side has hair follicles and the mouth side has minor salivary glands.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G118-18.jpg","in":"Stratified squamous epithelium on both sides of a deep cleft (C) beside a papilla of the tongue; the two arrows labeled B point to pale, oval, barrel-shaped groups of cells inside the epithelium (taste buds).","tg":["H15A slide 37 (The tongue)","H15A slide 36 (Gustatory pore)","H15A slide 39 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":18,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"4"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":1,"label":"Taste bud cells and microvilli in the pore","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-36.jpg","cap":"Taste bud diagram: gustatory pore, microvilli, support, gustatory and basal cells","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 36"},{"src":"../sl/G018-18.jpg","cap":"Taste bud: gustatory microvilli reach the taste pore; supporting and basal cells","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 18","lg":[["pink arrows","lead from each boxed area to its enlargement"],["orange arrow","taste buds (pale ovals) in the side wall of the papilla"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-26-1","s":"Name the structures numbered as #1, #2 and #3, respectively:","o":["A. Supporting cells; Cilia; Sensory cells","B. Supporting cells; Taste pore; Sensory cells","C. Goblet cells; Stereocilia; Supporting cells","D. Sensory cells; Microvilli; Basal cells","E. Basal cells; Taste pore; Sensory cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Sensory cells; Microvilli; Basal cells","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Sensory cells; microvilli; basal cells.</b> #1 marks the pale elongated <b>gustatory (sensory) cells</b> inside the bud. #2 marks their <b>microvilli</b> reaching into the taste pore. #3 marks a small <b>basal (stem) cell</b> at the base, above the nerve.<br>A. Supporting cells; cilia: #1 are sensory cells, and taste cells bear microvilli, not cilia.<br>B. Taste pore for #2: #2 points to the microvilli inside the pore.<br>C. Goblet cells; stereocilia: neither is in a taste bud.<br>E. Basal cells for #1: #1 is inside the bud, not at its base.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-51.jpg","in":"High-power taste bud: #1 on elongated pale cells inside the bud, #2 at the apical opening where processes project into the pore, #3 on a small cell at the bud base above the nerve.","tg":["H15A slide 36 (Gustatory pore)","H15A slide 37 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":51,"f":"G018"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":26,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":72,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":145,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["1","sensory (gustatory) cells: pale, round nuclei"],["2","gustatory microvilli in the taste pore"],["3","basal (stem) cell"]],"n":"5"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":2,"label":"Esophagus: nonkeratinized stratified squamous lining","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-55.jpg","cap":"Esophagus: thick nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 55"},{"src":"../sl/G018-23.jpg","cap":"Esophagus: squamous epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae, submucosal mucous glands","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 23","lg":[["SS","stratified squamous epithelium, nonkeratinized"],["LP","lamina propria"],["MM","muscularis mucosae (smooth muscle)"],["D","ducts of the esophageal glands"],["GL","esophageal mucous glands in the submucosa"]]},{"src":"../sl/G018-24.jpg","cap":"Esophagus transmural: squamous lining over inner circular and outer longitudinal muscle","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"G018-21-1","s":"From which organ could this section have been obtained?","o":["A. Outer surface of lip","B. Inner surface of lip","C. Tongue","D. Oral mucosa","E. Esophagus"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophagus","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Esophagus.</b> The lining is non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium. Under it sit a thin muscularis mucosae, a submucosa with a mucous gland, and then thick skeletal muscle in two layers at right angles (the inner layer cut along its length, the outer layer cut across). A muscularis externa of skeletal muscle in two neat layers is the upper third of the esophagus.<br>A. Outer surface of lip: thin skin, keratinized, with hair follicles and sweat glands.<br>B. Inner surface of lip: also non-keratinized, but it has no muscularis mucosae, and its skeletal muscle (orbicularis oris) is one sheet, not two layers at right angles; labial salivary glands sit under it.<br>C. Tongue: skeletal muscle bundles run in all three planes, and the top surface carries papillae.<br>D. Oral mucosa (cheek, floor of the mouth): no muscularis mucosae and no two-layer muscularis externa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-21.jpg","in":"Two pictures: low power of a wall with stratified squamous epithelium on one side, glands and vessels beneath, then two layers of skeletal muscle at right angles; high power of the non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium.","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)","H15A slide 53 (Esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":21,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"n":"6"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":3,"label":"Esophagus thirds: skeletal, mixed, smooth","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-60.jpg","cap":"Diagram: upper third skeletal, middle mixed, lower third smooth muscle","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 60"},{"src":"../sl/G015-59.jpg","cap":"Middle third: big skeletal fibers mixed with smooth muscle bundles","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 59"},{"src":"../sl/G018-22.jpg","cap":"Esophagus cross-section; text lists the muscularis externa by thirds","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-36-1","s":"What portion of the esophagus does this section corresponds to?","o":["A. Upper third","B. Middle third","C. Lower third"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Middle third","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Middle third.</b> The muscularis externa here <b>mixes large pink skeletal fibers</b> (nuclei at the edge of each fiber) <b>with bundles of small smooth muscle cells</b> (one central nucleus each). A mix of both types occurs only in the middle third.<br>A. Upper third: skeletal fibers only.<br>C. Lower third: smooth muscle only.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-52.jpg","in":"Muscularis externa with large pink cross-cut skeletal fibers (peripheral nuclei) mixed with smooth muscle bundles (central nuclei).","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":52,"f":"G018"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":36,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":76,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":151,"f":"F0052"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":221,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":1,"n":"7"},{"id":"G118-12-1","s":"Which segement of the esophagus is characterized by this type of muscularis externa?","o":["A. Top 1/3","B. Mid 1/3","C. Bottom 1/3"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Mid 1/3","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Mid 1/3.</b> One field shows both kinds of muscle, cut lengthwise. On the right are wide fibers with <b>cross-striations</b> and nuclei at their edges: skeletal muscle. On the left are spindle-shaped cells with <b>no striations</b> and long central nuclei: smooth muscle. The two are mixed only in the middle third of the esophagus.<br>A. Top 1/3: skeletal (striated) muscle only.<br>C. Bottom 1/3: smooth muscle only, like the rest of the digestive tube.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G118-12.jpg","in":"Muscularis externa cut lengthwise, H&E: on the right, wide fibers with cross-striations and nuclei at their edges (skeletal muscle); on the left, spindle cells with no striations and long central nuclei (smooth muscle). Both muscle types in one field.","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":12,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"8"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":4,"label":"Reflux esophagitis: symptoms, treatment, biopsy","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-57.jpg","cap":"Normal esophageal mucosa: squamous epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 57","lg":[["SS","nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium"],["LP","lamina propria"],["MM","muscularis mucosae (smooth muscle)"],["D","ducts of the esophageal glands"],["GL","esophageal mucous glands in the submucosa"]]},{"src":"../sl/G015-65.jpg","cap":"Reflux disease progression: normal lining, Barrett, dysplasia, invasive carcinoma","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 65"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-2-1","s":"A 35-year-old overweight man has had months of burning central chest pain after dinner, worse when he reclines, with a sour taste in his mouth. TUMS (an antacid) give some relief. He drinks 4–5 cups of coffee a day and a beer after dinner. What is the most likely diagnosis and best first management?","o":["A. Gastroesophageal reflux disease (reflux esophagitis): smaller evening meals, sitting upright after eating, less coffee and beer, a proton pump inhibitor, and a recheck in 8 weeks","B. Barrett esophagus: immediate esophagectomy","C. Eosinophilic esophagitis: an elimination diet and topical steroids","D. Achalasia: pneumatic dilation of the lower esophageal sphincter","E. Duodenal ulcer: Helicobacter pylori eradication therapy alone"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroesophageal reflux disease (reflux esophagitis): smaller evening meals, sitting upright after eating, less coffee and beer, a proton pump inhibitor, and a recheck in 8 weeks","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Gastroesophageal reflux disease (reflux esophagitis).</b> Burning chest pain after big meals while reclining, a sour taste and relief from antacids mean stomach acid is flowing back through a weak lower esophageal sphincter; coffee, alcohol and extra weight make it worse. First-line care is lifestyle change plus a proton pump inhibitor, with a recheck in 8 weeks.<br>B. Barrett esophagus: a later complication of long-standing reflux, found on biopsy; it is followed with surveillance endoscopy, not removed at once.<br>C. Eosinophilic esophagitis: considered only when reflux treatment fails; it is allergic and often makes food stick.<br>D. Achalasia: trouble swallowing both solids and liquids because the lower sphincter fails to relax, not acid heartburn relieved by antacids.<br>E. Duodenal ulcer: epigastric pain that eases with food, not burning chest pain on reclining after dinner.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":2,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":2,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"9"},{"id":"G017-8-1","s":"A 35-year-old man with months of heartburn after dinner has an upper endoscopy. The esophageal biopsy shows stratified squamous epithelium with a thickened basal cell layer, vascular papillae that reach up toward the surface, and scattered eosinophils within the epithelium. What is the diagnosis?","o":["A. Barrett esophagus","B. Reflux esophagitis","C. Esophageal adenocarcinoma","D. Candida esophagitis","E. Achalasia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Reflux esophagitis","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Reflux esophagitis.</b> Acid injury to the squamous lining gives three classic changes: basal cell hyperplasia (a thicker basal layer from faster turnover), elongated vascular papillae reaching toward the surface, and eosinophils inside the epithelium. The lining is still stratified squamous.<br>A. Barrett esophagus: the squamous lining is replaced by columnar epithelium with goblet cells.<br>C. Adenocarcinoma: invasive malignant glands, usually arising in Barrett mucosa.<br>D. Candida esophagitis: white plaques with yeast and fungal hyphae on the surface.<br>E. Achalasia: a motility disorder from loss of myenteric neurons; the lining is not the problem.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":8,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":6,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"10"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":4,"label":"Reflux esophagitis: differential diagnosis","slides":[],"q":[{"id":"G017-4-1","s":"A 35-year-old man treated for 8 weeks with a proton pump inhibitor for presumed reflux still has heartburn and now an occasional cough. Because most reflux responds to this treatment, other causes of esophagitis are considered. Which group lists those other causes?","o":["A. Eosinophilic esophagitis, infection (Candida, herpes simplex virus, cytomegalovirus), pill esophagitis and radiation injury","B. Achalasia, Zenker diverticulum and esophageal webs","C. Barrett esophagus, esophageal varices and Mallory-Weiss tears","D. Hiatal hernia, pyloric stenosis and duodenal ulcer","E. Celiac disease, Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Eosinophilic esophagitis, infection (Candida, herpes simplex virus, cytomegalovirus), pill esophagitis and radiation injury","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Eosinophilic esophagitis, infection, pill esophagitis and radiation injury.</b> Heartburn that does not settle on a proton pump inhibitor may not be acid reflux. The other causes of esophagitis are eosinophilic (allergic) esophagitis, infections (Candida; herpes simplex virus and cytomegalovirus, mostly in immunosuppressed people), pills that injure the lining (NSAIDs, doxycycline, ferrous sulfate) and radiation.<br>B. Achalasia, Zenker diverticulum, webs: these cause trouble swallowing, not inflammation of the lining.<br>C. Barrett esophagus, varices, Mallory-Weiss tears: Barrett is a complication of reflux, varices come from portal hypertension, and Mallory-Weiss tears follow forceful vomiting.<br>D. Hiatal hernia, pyloric stenosis, duodenal ulcer: a hiatal hernia worsens reflux rather than being another cause of esophagitis; the other two are stomach and duodenum problems.<br>E. Celiac disease, Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis: intestinal diseases, not causes of esophagitis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":4,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":3,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"11"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":4,"label":"Metaplasia: one mature epithelium replaces another","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-66.jpg","cap":"Metaplasia example: squamous esophagus replaced by columnar (Barrett) epithelium","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 66"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-12-1","s":"In cigarette smokers, ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium lining the airways may be replaced by stratified squamous epithelium. In chronic reflux esophagitis, stratified squamous epithelium lining the esophagus may be replaced by simple columnar epithelium. This replacement of the one epithelial type by another epithelial type in response to injury is referred to as:","o":["A. Neoplasia","B. Metaplasia","C. Dysplasia","D. Differentiation","E. Quiescence"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Metaplasia","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Metaplasia.</b> Metaplasia is the <b>replacement of one mature cell type by another</b> that better withstands an injury: squamous for ciliated cells in a smoker's airway, columnar for squamous in an acid-washed esophagus. It can reverse if the injury stops.<br>A. Neoplasia: uncontrolled new growth (a tumor).<br>C. Dysplasia: disordered, atypical growth; a precancer step after metaplasia.<br>D. Differentiation: normal maturation of a cell.<br>E. Quiescence: a resting, nondividing state.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":12,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":8,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"12"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":5,"label":"Heartburn plus red distal mucosa: Barrett esophagus","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-66.jpg","cap":"Normal squamous esophagus versus abnormal columnar Barrett mucosa","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 66"},{"src":"../sl/G015-65.jpg","cap":"Barrett diagram: goblet cells and glands replacing the squamous lining","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 65"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-10-1","s":"Three years after his reflux was controlled with a proton pump inhibitor, a man has a surveillance endoscopy. It shows salmon-colored tongues of mucosa extending up from the gastroesophageal junction into the pale squamous lining. The biopsy shows simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells where stratified squamous epithelium should be. What is the diagnosis?","o":["A. Reflux esophagitis","B. Barrett esophagus","C. Eosinophilic esophagitis","D. Hiatal hernia","E. Squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Barrett esophagus","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Barrett esophagus.</b> After years of reflux, salmon-colored tongues of simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells (intestinal metaplasia) replace the stratified squamous lining of the distal esophagus. It is a response to acid and raises the risk of adenocarcinoma, so it is followed with surveillance endoscopy.<br>A. Reflux esophagitis: the lining stays stratified squamous, with a thick basal layer and eosinophils.<br>C. Eosinophilic esophagitis: a squamous lining crowded with eosinophils.<br>D. Hiatal hernia: part of the stomach slides up through the diaphragm; the biopsy would show ordinary gastric mucosa without goblet cells.<br>E. Squamous cell carcinoma: invasive malignant squamous cells, usually in the middle esophagus, linked to smoking and alcohol.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 66 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":10,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":7,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"13"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":6,"label":"Stomach: simple columnar surface mucous cells","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-70.jpg","cap":"Gastric mucosa: simple columnar surface mucous cells (arrow), no goblet cells","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 70"},{"src":"../sl/G015-71.jpg","cap":"Gastric pits lined by surface mucous cells","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 71","lg":[["A","gastric pit lined by surface mucous cells"],["B","gastric gland starting at the base of the pit"],["inset","stomach surface seen by scanning electron microscope"],["P","gastric pit opening (inset)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G018-53-1","s":"Q6. Which cell type is indicated by the arrow?","o":["A. Enteroendocrine cell","B. Surface mucous cell","C. Enterocyte","D. Mucous neck cell","E. Chief cell"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Surface mucous cell","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Surface mucous cell.</b> The arrow is on the tall columnar cells that line the stomach surface and the gastric pits. Each cell has a pale, mucus-filled top, and the cells form one continuous sheet with no goblet cells between them. They make the thick, alkaline mucus layer that protects the lining from acid.<br>A. Enteroendocrine cell: small, scattered cells deep in the glands, with granules at the base; they need special stains to be seen.<br>C. Enterocyte: the absorptive cell of the intestine, with a brush border, sitting among goblet cells on villi.<br>D. Mucous neck cell: in the neck of the gastric gland, below the pit, not on the surface.<br>E. Chief cell: a purple (basophilic) cell at the base of the fundic glands.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-53.jpg","in":"Gastric surface and pits lined by tall columnar cells with pale apical cytoplasm; arrow on the surface lining.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)","H15A slide 71 (Gastric Pits)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":53,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["arrow","surface mucous cell (pale mucus cup at the apex, stomach surface)"]],"n":"14"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":7,"label":"Stomach regions: pit depth versus gland length","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-75.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture labels: cardia, body/fundus, pylorus (same pictures as the question)","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 75","lg":[["arrowhead (cardia panel)","where the pit's mucous lining meets the gland (neck)"],["P","deep gastric pits"],["G","short coiled pyloric glands (not G cells)"],["LP","lamina propria"]]},{"src":"../sl/G015-73.jpg","cap":"Cardia, body, pylorus glands: pits deepen and glands shorten toward pylorus","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 73"},{"src":"../sl/G018-34.jpg","cap":"Pyloric mucosa: deep pits over short coiled mucous glands","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"G118-5-1","s":"Identify the organ in the photomicrograph below.","o":["A. Esophagus","B. Stomach","C. Jejunum","D. Colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Stomach","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Stomach.</b> The surface is one sheet of tall, pale <b>surface mucous cells</b> that dips into <b>deep gastric pits</b>. Short, coiled, pale mucous glands lie under the pits, with the muscularis mucosae at the bottom. There are no goblet cells and no villi. Deep pits over coiled mucous glands is the look of the pyloric region.<br>A. Esophagus: thick stratified squamous epithelium, no pits.<br>C. Jejunum: villi covered by absorptive cells and goblet cells, on tall folds.<br>D. Colon: a flat surface over straight, tube-like crypts packed with goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G118-5.jpg","in":"Stomach mucosa, H&E: surface lined by a simple columnar epithelium of pale mucous cells that dips into deep pits; short coiled pale mucous glands under the pits (the look of the pyloric region); no goblet cells, no villi, no stratified squamous epithelium; muscularis mucosae at the bottom. Nothing is marked.","tg":["H15A slide 72 (SPECIAL FEATURES)","H15A slide 73 (Stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":5,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"15"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":8,"label":"Parietal pink, chief purple on H&E","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-78.jpg","cap":"Fundic glands, 400x: pale-pink parietal cells (PC) above purple chief cells (CC)","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 78","lg":[["box (50x)","area enlarged in the 100x panel"],["box (100x)","gland bases enlarged in the 400x panel"],["PC","parietal cells (pink, round central nucleus)"],["CC","chief cells (purple, at the gland bases)"]]},{"src":"../sl/G018-30.jpg","cap":"Fundus/body mucosa: pits, neck and glands; parietal (P) and chief (C) cells labelled","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 30","lg":[["GP","gastric pits"],["SM","surface mucous cells"],["MN","mucous neck cells"],["P","parietal cells (big, pink, round 'fried egg')"],["C","chief cells (purple, basophilic, deep in the glands)"],["MM","muscularis mucosae"],["boxes","areas enlarged at right"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G018-54-1","s":"Q7. The cell indicated by A produces:","o":["A. Pepsinogen","B. Intrinsic factor","C. Histamine","D. Gastrin","E. Lysozyme"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Intrinsic factor","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Intrinsic factor.</b> Cell A is large and round, stains pale pink (acidophilic, because it is full of mitochondria), and has a round central nucleus: a parietal (oxyntic) cell of a fundic gland. Parietal cells secrete hydrochloric acid (HCl) and intrinsic factor, which is needed to absorb vitamin B12. (Cell B is a purple chief cell.)<br>A. Pepsinogen: made by chief cells (cell B), not by cell A.<br>C. Histamine: made by enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells, a type of enteroendocrine cell.<br>D. Gastrin: made by G cells in the pyloric (antral) glands.<br>E. Lysozyme: made by Paneth cells of the small intestine (and by pyloric gland cells).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-54.jpg","in":"Fundic glands: arrow A on a round pink cell with a central nucleus; arrow B on a purple (basophilic) cell lower in the gland.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":54,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["A","parietal cell (pink, round, central nucleus): HCl and intrinsic factor"],["B","chief cell (purple, basophilic, gland base): pepsinogen"]],"n":"16"},{"id":"G118-6-1","s":"Which of the following is secreted by the cells at the tips of the arrows?","o":["A. Pepsinogen","B. Secretin","C. CCK","D. HCL"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"HCL","a":"D","why":"<b>D. HCl (hydrochloric acid).</b> The arrows are on large, round, <b>pale pink (acidophilic)</b> cells with a round central nucleus in the gastric glands: <b>parietal cells</b>. Parietal cells secrete hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor.<br>A. Pepsinogen: made by chief cells, the smaller blue-purple cells lying between the pink ones.<br>B. Secretin: a hormone from enteroendocrine cells of the small intestine; those cells are small, pale and scattered.<br>C. CCK (cholecystokinin): also a hormone from enteroendocrine cells of the small intestine, not a product of the gastric glands.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G118-6.jpg","in":"Base of gastric (fundic) glands, H&E: four arrows on large, round, pale pink (acidophilic) cells with a central round nucleus (parietal cells); smaller blue-purple cells (chief cells) lie between them; muscularis mucosae at the bottom.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)","H15A slide 79 (Parietal Cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":6,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"17"},{"id":"G118-7-1","s":"Identify the cells at the tip of the arrows?","o":["A. Chief cells","B. Enteroendocrine cells","C. Parietal cells","D. Mucous cells"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Chief cells","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Chief cells.</b> The arrows are on the smaller, <b>blue-purple (basophilic)</b> cells squeezed between the large pink cells of the gastric glands. The blue colour comes from their rough endoplasmic reticulum, which makes pepsinogen.<br>B. Enteroendocrine cells: small, pale and scattered; they are hard to see without special stains.<br>C. Parietal cells: the large, round, pink cells with a central nucleus that surround the arrowed cells.<br>D. Mucous cells: pale, foamy cells on the surface, in the pits and in the gland necks, not dark blue cells deep in the gland.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G118-7.jpg","in":"Same field of gastric (fundic) glands as Kahoot question 6, but the four arrows are now on the small blue-purple (basophilic) cells that lie between the large pink parietal cells (chief cells).","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)","H15A slide 78 (50x)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":7,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"18"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":9,"label":"Enteroendocrine cells: special stains, serotonin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G018-31.jpg","cap":"Enteroendocrine cells: seen with chromium, silver or immunostains; serotonin increases motility","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 31"},{"src":"../sl/G018-32.jpg","cap":"Electron micrograph: enteroendocrine cell (E) with basal granules beside parietal, chief cells","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-13-1","s":"A researcher uses an immunostaining preparation to examine a slide from the gastric cardia mucosa. On his immunostained preparation, he can observe scattered cells in the glandular portion not seen in the H&E preparation. Which of the following best applies to these cells?","o":["A. Their secretions may increase motility of the GI tract","B. They ordinarily replace old or damaged gastric epithelial cells","C. They are capable of secreting mucus in the stomach","D. They are required for vitamin B12 absorption","E. Intracellular canaliculi are an ultrastructural feature of these cells"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Their secretions may increase motility of the GI tract","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Their secretions may increase gut motility.</b> Cells seen only with special stains (immunostaining, silver or chromium salts) are <b>enteroendocrine cells</b>. One type, the enterochromaffin cell, secretes <b>serotonin</b>, which increases motility.<br>B. Replacing old cells: stem cells in the isthmus and neck.<br>C. Secreting mucus: surface mucous and mucous neck cells, visible on routine hematoxylin and eosin stain.<br>D. Needed for vitamin B12 absorption: parietal cells (intrinsic factor).<br>E. Intracellular canaliculi: parietal cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Immunostain of gastric glands with scattered dark brown-stained single cells in the glandular epithelium (enteroendocrine cells); not needed to answer.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":13,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":9,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"19"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":10,"label":"Pernicious anemia: no B12, large red cells","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-85.jpg","cap":"Pernicious anemia: no parietal-cell intrinsic factor, so B12 deficiency and large red cells","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 85"},{"src":"../sl/G017-17.jpg","cap":"Smear: hypersegmented neutrophil, oval macrocytes and Howell-Jolly body in B12 deficiency","where":"2026 digestive interactive, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-21-1","s":"In pernicious anemia, the parietal cells no longer secrete intrinsic factor. Which vitamin becomes deficient, and what type of anemia results?","o":["A. Folate; microcytic anemia","B. Vitamin B12; megaloblastic (macrocytic) anemia","C. Iron; microcytic hypochromic anemia","D. Vitamin B6; sideroblastic anemia","E. Vitamin B12; microcytic anemia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin B12; megaloblastic (macrocytic) anemia","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Vitamin B12; megaloblastic anemia.</b> Parietal cells secrete intrinsic factor, which binds vitamin B12 so the terminal ileum can absorb it. Without it, B12 falls, DNA synthesis in the marrow slows, and red cells come out large (megaloblastic).<br>A. Folate, microcytic: folate is absorbed without intrinsic factor, and its deficiency also makes large, not small, cells.<br>C. Iron, microcytic: low acid can reduce iron absorption, but the missing intrinsic factor is the defining defect.<br>D. Vitamin B6, sideroblastic: B6 is needed for heme synthesis and has nothing to do with intrinsic factor.<br>E. Vitamin B12, microcytic: the vitamin is right, but B12 lack gives large cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)","H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":21,"f":"G017"}],"tier":1,"n":"20"},{"id":"G017-16-1","s":"A 60-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes and Hashimoto thyroiditis has fatigue, numbness and tingling, and unsteadiness. Hemoglobin is 10 g/dL and MCV (mean corpuscular volume) is 110 fL. Her peripheral blood smear is shown. Which findings does it show?","o":["A. Small pale red cells with central pallor wider than one-third of the cell (iron deficiency)","B. Oval macrocytes, a hypersegmented neutrophil and a Howell-Jolly body (megaloblastic anemia)","C. Sickled red cells and target cells (sickle cell disease)","D. Spherocytes without central pallor (hereditary spherocytosis)","E. Schistocytes (fragmented red cells) from microangiopathic hemolysis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Oval macrocytes, a hypersegmented neutrophil and a Howell-Jolly body (megaloblastic anemia)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Oval macrocytes, a hypersegmented neutrophil and a Howell-Jolly body.</b> Big oval red cells, a neutrophil with too many nuclear lobes and a small nuclear leftover inside a red cell mean megaloblastic anemia. With an MCV of 110 fL in a woman with other autoimmune diseases, the cause is vitamin B12 deficiency from pernicious anemia.<br>A. Iron deficiency: small, pale cells and a low MCV.<br>C. Sickle cell disease: curved, sickled cells; it is inherited, not autoimmune.<br>D. Hereditary spherocytosis: small round cells without central pallor and a normal or low MCV.<br>E. Schistocytes: cell fragments from damage in small vessels, as in hemolytic uremic syndrome or thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G017-16.jpg","in":"Peripheral blood smear with large oval red cells, a neutrophil with many nuclear lobes, and a red cell containing a small dark nuclear remnant (Howell-Jolly body).","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":16,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":12,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"21"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":10,"label":"Pernicious anemia: parietal cells lost","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-76.jpg","cap":"Parietal (oxyntic) cells secrete HCl and intrinsic factor for vitamin B12 absorption","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 76"},{"src":"../sl/G015-85.jpg","cap":"Pernicious anemia: intrinsic factor lost; stomach bypass surgery can also cause it","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 85"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-19-1","s":"A 60-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes and Hashimoto thyroiditis has megaloblastic anemia from low vitamin B12. Endoscopy shows diffuse atrophic gastritis of the fundus and body; Helicobacter pylori is negative. Which pattern of gastritis and which antibodies fit this autoimmune pernicious anemia?","o":["A. Antrum-predominant gastritis; antibodies to gastrin-secreting G cells","B. Atrophic autoimmune gastritis of the fundus and body; antibodies to the parietal cell H⁺/K⁺-ATPase (proton pump) and to intrinsic factor","C. Helicobacter pylori gastritis of the antrum; antibodies to urease","D. Acute erosive gastritis; antibodies to chief cell pepsinogen","E. Atrophic gastritis of the fundus and body; antibodies to tissue transglutaminase"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Atrophic autoimmune gastritis of the fundus and body; antibodies to the parietal cell H⁺/K⁺-ATPase (proton pump) and to intrinsic factor","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Atrophic autoimmune gastritis; antibodies to the H⁺/K⁺-ATPase and to intrinsic factor.</b> The immune attack targets the fundus and body, where parietal cells live. Losing them means no acid and no intrinsic factor, so vitamin B12 is not absorbed. It travels with other autoimmune diseases such as type 1 diabetes and Hashimoto thyroiditis.<br>A. Antral gastritis with G-cell antibodies: the antrum is spared in autoimmune gastritis, and gastrin rises because acid is low.<br>C. Helicobacter pylori gastritis: mostly antral, with no autoantibodies; she tested negative.<br>D. Acute erosive gastritis: from NSAIDs, alcohol or stress, with no autoantibodies.<br>E. Tissue transglutaminase antibodies: the marker of celiac disease, a small-intestine disease.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)","H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":19,"f":"G017"}],"tier":1,"n":"22"},{"id":"G017-23-1","s":"[Case context, slides 16-18: 60-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes and Hashimoto thyroiditis; fatigue, worsening numbness/tingling, unsteadiness, foul-smelling diarrhea, pale conjunctiva, HR 100; Hb 10, MCV 110 fL; low vitamin B12; EGD: diffuse atrophic gastritis of fundus and body, H. pylori negative, autoimmune pattern.] An image from the gastric body biopsy is presented below with an image from a more normal gastric body for comparison. Absence of which cell type in the patient's gastric body is most likely responsible for her clinical findings?","o":["A. Chief cells","B. Parietal cells","C. Surface mucous cells","D. Enteroendocrine cells","E. Mucous neck cells"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Parietal cells","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Parietal cells.</b> Autoimmune atrophic gastritis destroys the <b>parietal cells</b> of the fundus and body. They make <b>intrinsic factor</b>, so vitamin B12 is not absorbed: megaloblastic anemia plus nerve damage (numbness, unsteadiness).<br>A. Chief cells: make pepsinogen; losing them does not cause B12 deficiency.<br>C. Surface mucous cells: protective alkaline mucus.<br>D. Enteroendocrine cells: hormones such as gastrin and serotonin.<br>E. Mucous neck cells: acidic mucus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Biopsy (chronic atrophic gastritis): thin mucosa with few, sparse glands and inflamed lamina propria vs normal body mucosa packed with tall oxyntic glands.","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)","H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":23,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":15,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"23"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":11,"label":"Micrograph: fold with submucosal core = plica","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G016-21.jpg","cap":"Jejunum: prominent plicae circulares (P) with thin submucosal cores (SM)","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 21","lg":[["V","villi covering each fold"],["P","plica circularis (circular fold)"],["SM","submucosa (blue-green core of each fold)"],["M","muscularis externa (inner circular, outer longitudinal)"],["S","serosa"]]},{"src":"../sl/G021-16.jpg","cap":"Definitions: plica has submucosal core; villus has lamina propria core; microvilli apical","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"G018-58-1","s":"Q11. Name the circled structure:","o":["A. Plicae circularis","B. Brush border (microvilli)","C. Villi","D. Rugae"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Plicae circularis","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Plicae circularis.</b> The circle holds a whole fold that has a core of submucosa (connective tissue) and is covered with many villi. Plicae circulares (valves of Kerckring) are permanent circular folds of mucosa plus submucosa: the largest of the three ways the small intestine enlarges its surface (plicae, then villi, then microvilli).<br>B. Brush border (microvilli): far too small to see at this power; it is the striped edge on top of each absorptive cell.<br>C. Villi: the many finger-like projections on the surface of the fold; each has only a lamina propria core.<br>D. Rugae: folds of the stomach wall, which flatten out when the stomach fills.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-58.jpg","in":"Low power small intestine; an oval circles one entire tall fold (with a connective-tissue core) covered by villi.","tg":["H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)","H15B slide 10 (Digests food and absorbs end products of digestion.)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":58,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["circle","plica circularis: mucosa fold with a submucosa core, covered by villi"]],"n":"24"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":11,"label":"Micrograph: finger-like projection = villus","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G018-37.jpg","cap":"Light-microscope level: intestinal villi with lamina propria cores","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 37"},{"src":"../sl/G021-16.jpg","cap":"Villi: finger-like fronds of columnar epithelium around a lamina propria core","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"G018-59-1","s":"Q12. Name the circled structure:","o":["A. Plicae circularis","B. MicrovillIi","C. Villus","D. Rugae"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Villus","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Villus.</b> The circle holds one finger-like projection of mucosa: epithelium wrapped around a core of lamina propria, sticking out from the side of a larger fold. Villi are the middle size of the three surface enlargements of the small intestine.<br>A. Plicae circularis: the whole large fold that carries the villi; its core is submucosa.<br>B. Microvilli: tiny projections of the cell membrane, seen only as the brush border at high power.<br>D. Rugae: stomach folds, not found in the small intestine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-59.jpg","in":"Medium power: one fold with many finger-like projections; an oval (and arrow) on a single projection.","tg":["H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)","H15B slide 10 (Digests food and absorbs end products of digestion.)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":59,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["circle and arrow","one villus: finger of lamina propria covered by enterocytes"]],"n":"25"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":11,"label":"Brush border = microvilli","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G018-39.jpg","cap":"Brush border on enterocytes, and its microvilli by electron microscopy","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 39"}],"q":[{"id":"G018-60-1","s":"Q13. Name the structure shown by the arrow:","o":["A. Plicae circularis","B. Brush border (microvilli)","C. Villi","D. Rugae"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Brush border (microvilli)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Brush border (microvilli).</b> The arrow points to the thin, pink, finely striped band on the top (apical) surface of the columnar absorptive cells (enterocytes). It is made of thousands of microvilli on each cell and carries the brush-border enzymes for the last steps of digestion.<br>A. Plicae circularis: large folds with a submucosal core, seen at low power.<br>C. Villi: finger-like projections made of many cells, not a band on top of single cells.<br>D. Rugae: folds of the stomach wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-60.jpg","in":"High power of a villus tip: arrow on the pink apical border of the columnar cells.","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 12 (Mucosa features)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":60,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["arrow","brush border (microvilli) on enterocytes"]],"n":"26"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":12,"label":"Enterocytes: terminal digestion and absorption","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G018-38.jpg","cap":"Enterocyte microvilli enlarge surface for terminal digestion and absorption","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 38"},{"src":"../sl/G018-39.jpg","cap":"Enterocytes: brush border of microvilli by light and electron microscopy","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 39"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-30-1","s":"A sample of small intestinal mucosa from a patient with celiac disease and a sample of normal small intestinal mucosa for comparison are illustrated below. Given the histologically apparent structural alterations of the small intestinal mucosa seen here (celiac disease: villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia), what normal functions would you expect to be most abnormal in the patient with celiac disease?","o":["A. Concentration of bile","B. Terminal digestion and nutrient absorption","C. Water resorption","D. Initiation of carbohydrate digestion","E. Initiation of protein and triglyceride digestion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Terminal digestion and nutrient absorption","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Terminal digestion and nutrient absorption.</b> Celiac disease flattens the villi (<b>villous atrophy</b>) and deepens the crypts. Fewer villi and microvilli mean less surface and fewer <b>brush-border enzymes</b> on enterocytes, so the last steps of digestion and absorption fail.<br>A. Concentrating bile: the gallbladder.<br>C. Water resorption: mostly the colon.<br>D. Starting carbohydrate digestion: salivary amylase in the mouth.<br>E. Starting protein and triglyceride digestion: pepsin and gastric lipase in the stomach.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Celiac mucosa: flat surface with no villi and long hyperplastic crypts; normal mucosa: tall villi over short crypts (labels in stem).","tg":["H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)","H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":30,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":24,"f":"F0702"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":48,"f":"F3961"}],"tier":1,"n":"27"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":13,"label":"Paneth granules: lysozyme and defensins","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G018-38.jpg","cap":"Paneth cells: innate immunity; granules release lysozyme, defensins, phospholipase A2","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 38"},{"src":"../sl/G016-18.jpg","cap":"Crypt base: Paneth cells packed with bright red apical granules (arrow)","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 18","lg":[["arrow","Paneth cell granules (large, bright red, toward the lumen)"],["arrowhead","enteroendocrine cell (pale, finely granular cytoplasm, wedged between crypt cells)"],["M","mast cell in the lamina propria (packed with purple-red granules)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G017-27-1","s":"The granules within the cells at the pointer are rich in which of the following substances?","o":["A. Defensins","B. Cholecystokinin","C. Pepsin","D. IgA","E. Mucin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Defensins","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Defensins.</b> Cells at the <b>crypt base</b> packed with <b>bright red (eosinophilic) apical granules</b> are <b>Paneth cells</b>. Their granules release <b>defensins, lysozyme and phospholipase A2</b>, which kill bacteria (innate immunity).<br>B. Cholecystokinin: from enteroendocrine cells.<br>C. Pepsin: made as pepsinogen by stomach chief cells.<br>D. IgA (immunoglobulin A): from plasma cells in the lamina propria.<br>E. Mucin: from goblet cells, which look pale, not red.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G017-27.jpg","in":"High-power crypt base of small intestine; arrows point to cells packed with bright red/magenta apical granules (Paneth cells).","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 17 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":27,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":21,"f":"F0702"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":24,"f":"F3961"},{"t":"2023 clicker","n":"CL10 digestive system clickers","sl":17,"f":"F0731"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Digestive system review 2023","sl":19,"f":"F0744"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["arrows","Paneth cell granules (bright red, crypt base; defensins, lysozyme)"]],"n":"28"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":13,"label":"Paneth cells: red granules at crypt base","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G016-17.jpg","cap":"Crypt base: Paneth cells with red granules (A); circled cell is dividing","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 17","lg":[["A (arrowheads)","Paneth cells: big red granules (lysozyme, defensins) at the crypt base"],["circle","dividing cell (mitotic figure: clumped chromosomes with a clear halo); crypts are where new epithelial cells are made"]]},{"src":"../sl/G016-18.jpg","cap":"Paneth cells: crypt-base cells packed with bright red apical granules","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 18","lg":[["arrow","Paneth cell granules (large, bright red, toward the lumen)"],["arrowhead","enteroendocrine cell (pale, finely granular cytoplasm, wedged between crypt cells)"],["M","mast cell in the lamina propria (packed with purple-red granules)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G018-55-1","s":"Q8. The granules in the cell indicated by the letter “A” contain:","o":["A. Pepsinogen","B. Intrinsic factor","C. Histamine","D. Gastrin","E. Lysozyme"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Lysozyme","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Lysozyme.</b> Cell A sits at the base of a crypt of Lieberkühn and is packed with large, bright red (eosinophilic) granules: a Paneth cell. Its granules hold antibacterial proteins: lysozyme, defensins and phospholipase A2. (The circled cell is a dividing stem cell.)<br>A. Pepsinogen: chief cells of the stomach.<br>B. Intrinsic factor: parietal cells of the stomach.<br>C. Histamine: enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells of the stomach.<br>D. Gastrin: G cells of the pyloric antrum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-55.jpg","in":"Base of an intestinal crypt: cell A filled with large bright red granules; a mitotic figure is circled nearby.","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 17 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":55,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["A (arrowheads)","Paneth cells: big red granules (lysozyme, defensins) at the crypt base"],["circle","dividing cell (mitotic figure: clumped chromosomes with a clear halo); crypts are where new epithelial cells are made"]],"n":"29"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":14,"label":"Brunner glands → duodenum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G016-19.jpg","cap":"Duodenum: Brunner's glands fill the submucosa below the muscularis mucosae","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 19","lg":[["dashed line","boundary between villi (above) and intestinal crypts (below)"],["arrow","Brunner gland duct crossing the muscularis mucosae into a crypt"]]},{"src":"../sl/G017-25.jpg","cap":"Brunner gland mucus, pH eight to nine-and-a-half, neutralizes acid chyme","where":"2026 digestive interactive, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-24-1","s":"Histological evaluation of a portion of the gastrointestinal tract from a 55-year-old man with long-term gastrointestinal complaints shows ramified, tubular glands located in the submucosal layer. The glands contain secretions with pH close to 9.0. Which of the following segments of the gastrointestinal tract matches these findings?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Jejunum","D. Ileum","E. Ascending colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Duodenum.</b> Branched tubular glands in the <b>submucosa</b> that make <b>alkaline mucus (pH near nine)</b> are <b>Brunner glands</b>. They neutralize acid chyme from the stomach and, among intestinal segments, exist only in the duodenum.<br>A. Stomach: its glands lie in the mucosa and secrete acid.<br>C. Jejunum: thin submucosa with no glands; tall plicae.<br>D. Ileum: Peyer's patches, no submucosal glands.<br>E. Ascending colon: crypts in the mucosa only.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Small intestine with villi (mucosa) above and lobules of pale branched glands filling the submucosa (Brunner glands); stem gives the finding.","tg":["H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)","H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":24,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":18,"f":"F0702"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":3,"f":"F3961"},{"t":"2023 clicker","n":"CL10 digestive system clickers","sl":2,"f":"F0731"}],"tier":1,"n":"30"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":14,"label":"Micrograph: submucosal glands → duodenum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G016-19.jpg","cap":"Duodenum: Brunner's glands fill the submucosa below the muscularis mucosae","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 19","lg":[["dashed line","boundary between villi (above) and intestinal crypts (below)"],["arrow","Brunner gland duct crossing the muscularis mucosae into a crypt"]]},{"src":"../sl/G018-36.jpg","cap":"Three segments: duodenum Brunner glands, jejunum plicae, ileum Peyer's patches","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 36","lg":[["dashed line","border between villi (above) and crypts (below)"],["arrow","Brunner gland duct emptying into the base of a crypt"],["P","plica circularis (fold with a submucosa core)"],["SM","submucosa"],["M","muscularis externa"],["large box","one Peyer patch lymphoid nodule with the mucosa over it"],["small box","edge of a lymphoid nodule against the submucosa"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G018-56-1","s":"Q9. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Jejunum","D. Ileum","E. Colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Duodenum.</b> The surface has villi, so this is small intestine, and the submucosa is filled with a thick layer of pale mucous glands: the Brunner (submucosal) glands. Brunner glands are found only in the duodenum; their alkaline mucus helps neutralize the acid coming from the stomach.<br>A. Stomach: gastric pits and glands, no villi.<br>C. Jejunum: tall plicae and villi, but no glands in the submucosa.<br>D. Ileum: Peyer patches (large lymphoid nodules), no submucosal glands.<br>E. Colon: no villi; straight crypts full of goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-56.jpg","in":"Low power: villous mucosa with a broad band of pale glands below the muscularis mucosae, then muscularis externa.","tg":["H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)","H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":56,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"n":"31"},{"id":"G118-4-1","s":"Which segment of the GI tract is presented in the image below?","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Jejunum","C. Ileum","D. Colon"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Duodenum.</b> The arrows point to large lobules of pale, coiled mucous glands that fill the <b>submucosa</b>, between the mucosa with its crypts (left) and the muscularis externa (lower right). These are <b>Brunner glands</b>, found only in the duodenum. Their alkaline mucus neutralizes the acid arriving from the stomach.<br>B. Jejunum: a thin submucosa with no glands; tall plicae.<br>C. Ileum: Peyer patches (large groups of lymphoid nodules), no submucosal glands.<br>D. Colon: no villi and no submucosal glands; straight crypts full of goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G118-4.jpg","in":"Wall of the small intestine, H&E: darker mucosa with crypts on the left; three arrows from a blacked-out label point to large lobules of pale, coiled glands that fill the submucosa (Brunner's glands); muscularis externa at lower right.","tg":["H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":4,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"32"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":14,"label":"Micrograph: tall plicae, no glands → jejunum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G016-21.jpg","cap":"Jejunum: prominent plicae circulares, thin non-remarkable submucosa","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 21","lg":[["V","villi covering each fold"],["P","plica circularis (circular fold)"],["SM","submucosa (blue-green core of each fold)"],["M","muscularis externa (inner circular, outer longitudinal)"],["S","serosa"]]},{"src":"../sl/G018-36.jpg","cap":"Three segments: duodenum Brunner glands, jejunum prominent plicae, ileum Peyer's patches","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 36","lg":[["dashed line","border between villi (above) and crypts (below)"],["arrow","Brunner gland duct emptying into the base of a crypt"],["P","plica circularis (fold with a submucosa core)"],["SM","submucosa"],["M","muscularis externa"],["large box","one Peyer patch lymphoid nodule with the mucosa over it"],["small box","edge of a lymphoid nodule against the submucosa"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G018-57-1","s":"Q10. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Jejunum","D. Ileum","E. Colon"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Jejunum.</b> Tall, well-developed plicae circulares (folds with a submucosal core), each covered with villi. The submucosa is thin and has no glands, and the lymphoid nodules are small. Plicae are most developed in the jejunum.<br>A. Stomach: rugae and gastric pits, no villi.<br>B. Duodenum: would show Brunner glands in the submucosa.<br>D. Ileum: would show large Peyer patches; its plicae are smaller.<br>E. Colon: no villi or plicae; straight crypts with goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-57.jpg","in":"Low power: several tall folds of mucosa with a submucosal core, each covered by villi.","tg":["H15B slide 21 (Prominent plicae circulares)","H15B slide 22 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":57,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"n":"33"},{"id":"G118-10-1","s":"The structute indicated by the bracket is characteristic of which organ?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Jejunum","C. Colon","D. Appendix"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Jejunum.</b> The bracket spans one whole tall fold with a pale core of submucosa, covered by many villi: a <b>plica circularis</b>. Plicae are tallest and most numerous in the jejunum. Here the submucosa is thin, with no glands and no large lymphoid nodules.<br>A. Stomach: its folds (rugae) are covered by gastric pits, not villi.<br>C. Colon: no plicae and no villi; a flat surface over straight crypts.<br>D. Appendix: a small lumen ringed by lymphoid follicles; no villi and no plicae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G118-10.jpg","in":"Small intestine, H&E, low power: tall permanent folds with a pale core of submucosa, each covered with many villi; the bracket spans one whole fold (plica circularis); thin submucosa with no glands and no lymphoid nodules.","tg":["H15B slide 21 (Prominent plicae circulares)","H15B slide 22 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":10,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"34"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":15,"label":"Micrograph: crypts, goblet cells, no villi → colon","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G016-28.jpg","cap":"Colon mucosa: flat surface, straight glands (Gl) full of goblet cells, no villi","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 28","lg":[["arrows","gland openings on the flat surface"],["box (upper right)","surface epithelium and gland tops"],["Gl","straight intestinal glands (crypts), full of goblet cells"],["LP","lamina propria"],["box (lower left)","gland bases"],["Muc","mucosa"],["SubM","submucosa"],["MM","muscularis mucosae"]]},{"src":"../sl/G016-27.jpg","cap":"Colon mucosa: straight crypts packed with goblet cells, lymphoid tissue below","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"G018-61-1","s":"Q14. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Ileum","D. Colon","E. Jejunum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Colon.</b> The surface is flat with no villi, and the mucosa is packed with straight tubular crypts full of pale goblet cells. That pattern is the large intestine, whose main jobs are absorbing water and making mucus.<br>A. Stomach: gastric pits leading into glands with parietal and chief cells, few goblet cells.<br>B. Duodenum: villi plus Brunner glands in the submucosa.<br>C. Ileum: villi plus Peyer patches.<br>E. Jejunum: villi on tall plicae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-61.jpg","in":"Mucosa of straight tubular glands crowded with pale goblet cells; flat surface; muscularis mucosae and submucosa below.","tg":["H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)","H15B slide 27 (Colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":61,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"n":"35"},{"id":"G118-3-1","s":"Which of the following features is characteristic of the organ presented?","o":["A. Rugae","B. Colonocytes","C. Vili","D. Peyer's Patches"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Colonocytes","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Colonocytes.</b> The picture shows a flat surface with no villi and straight, tube-like crypts packed with pale goblet cells: the <b>colon</b>. Colonocytes are the colon's columnar absorptive cells; they take up water and electrolytes.<br>A. Rugae: the large folds of the stomach, which has gastric pits and no goblet cells.<br>C. Villi: finger-like projections of the small intestine; the colon has none.<br>D. Peyer's patches: large groups of lymphoid nodules in the ileum, which also has villi.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G118-3.jpg","in":"Colon mucosa, H&E: flat surface with no villi; straight tubular crypts of Lieberkühn packed with pale goblet cells; muscularis mucosae at the bottom. Nothing is marked: the organ has to be recognized first.","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":3,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"36"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":16,"label":"Teniae coli: colon's outer longitudinal muscle","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G016-26.jpg","cap":"Large intestine tunics: outer longitudinal muscularis forms the tenia coli","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 26"},{"src":"../sl/G018-43.jpg","cap":"Large intestine: outer muscularis in three longitudinal ribbons, the taenia coli","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 43"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-33-1","s":"In the gastrointestinal tract, the muscularis propria consists of an inner circular layer and an outer longitudinal layer of smooth muscle with an intervening neural plexus (Auerbach). Exceptions to this general plan are seen in the stomach where an inner oblique layer of smooth muscle is added and in a second location where the outer layer is condensed into three longitudinal ribbons (taenia) of smooth muscle. Where can we expect to find this modified organization and what plane of section would be required to demonstrate it?","o":["A. Esophagus, longitudinal section","B. Ileum, transverse section","C. Colon, longitudinal section","D. Colon, transverse section","E. Anal canal, external sphincter, transverse section"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Colon, transverse section","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Colon, transverse section.</b> In the colon, the outer longitudinal muscle gathers into <b>three bands, the teniae coli</b>. A <b>cross (transverse) section</b> shows all three as separate thick bundles around the wall, with thin muscle between them.<br>A. Esophagus: complete outer longitudinal layer, no teniae.<br>B. Ileum: complete outer longitudinal layer.<br>C. Colon, longitudinal section: right organ, but a lengthwise cut shows at most one strip, not three bands.<br>E. External anal sphincter: a ring of skeletal muscle, not teniae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Eroschenko drawing of a transverse section of colon wall with intestinal glands, muscularis externa and a boxed label 'Taeniae coli' pointing at the thickened outer longitudinal band.","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 34 (Appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":33,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":25,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"37"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":17,"label":"Identify a striated duct by its basal striations","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G020-13.jpg","cap":"Striated duct (SD) among serous acini, with drawing of basal striations","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 13","lg":[["a","light micrograph, hematoxylin and eosin"],["SD","striated duct (faint basal striations)"],["b","scanning electron micrograph, duct cells seen from the side"],["L","lumen"],["B","basal ends with interlocking membrane folds"],["c","scanning electron micrograph of the cell bases (basal lamina removed)"]]},{"src":"../sl/G024-23.jpg","cap":"Striated duct (SD): pale columnar cells with basal striations among serous acini","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 23","lg":[["A","serous acini (dark, granular)"],["M","mucous cells (pale, foamy)"],["S","serous demilunes (dark crescents capping mucous tubules)"],["arrows (top edge)","more serous demilunes (their S labels cropped off)"],["ID","small intralobular duct"],["SD","striated duct (pale columnar cells, basal striations)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-186-1","s":"Question 3 Name the highlighted structure:","o":["A. Serous gland","B. Mucous gland","C. Striated duct","D. Mixed gland","E. Excretory duct"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Striated duct","a":"C","why":"C. A ring of <b>tall pink columnar cells</b> with central round nuclei and <b>vertical stripes in the basal cytoplasm</b>, around a small lumen inside the lobule, is a <b>striated duct</b>.<br>A. A serous acinus is dark granular pyramidal cells around a tiny lumen.<br>B. Mucous cells are pale and foamy, with flat basal nuclei.<br>D. A mixed gland is a whole organ, not one circled tube.<br>E. Excretory (interlobular) ducts are larger and lie in connective-tissue septa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/F0052-186.jpg","in":"High-power H&E: a green outline circles a duct of tall columnar cells with central round nuclei and radially striated pale basal cytoplasm around a small lumen, among dark serous acini.","tg":["H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":49,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":186,"f":"F0052"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":4,"f":"F3959"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["green outline","striated duct (pink columnar cells, central round nuclei, basal striations)"]],"n":"38"},{"id":"G118-16-1","s":"Identify the structure in the yellow outline.","o":["A. Pancreatic duct","B. Interlobular duct","C. Intercalated duct","D. Striated duct"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Striated duct","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Striated duct.</b> The outlined tube lies <b>inside the lobule</b>, among the acini, and is wider than they are. Its wall is one layer of <b>tall pink columnar cells with round central nuclei</b> around an open lumen. The pale mucous acini with dark serous caps at the top show that this is a mixed salivary gland.<br>A. Pancreatic duct: the pancreas has no mucous acini and no striated ducts; its ducts are lined by pale cells.<br>B. Interlobular duct: lies in a connective tissue septum between lobules, not among the acini.<br>C. Intercalated duct: much smaller, with low cuboidal cells and a narrow lumen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G118-16.jpg","in":"Mixed salivary gland, H&E: dark serous acini, a few pale mucous acini with demilunes at the top; the yellow circle surrounds a large duct inside the lobule, lined by tall, pale pink columnar cells with round central nuclei around an open lumen (striated duct).","tg":["H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)","H16 slide 12 (Ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":16,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"39"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":18,"label":"Interlobular duct = duct in the septum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G020-16.jpg","cap":"Sublingual atlas plate: large interlobular excretory duct running in a connective-tissue septum","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 16"},{"src":"../sl/G020-28.jpg","cap":"Labelled pancreas: interlobular duct in a septum, intercalated duct, centroacinar cells, islets","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 28"},{"src":"../sl/F0754-205.jpg","cap":"Duct table: interlobular ducts lie between lobules, within the septa","where":"2022 TA review, slide 205"}],"q":[{"id":"F3959-9-1","s":"Which structure is shown by the arrow?","o":["A. Centroacinar duct","B. Intralobular duct","C. Striated duct","D. Interlobular duct","E. Intercalated duct"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Interlobular duct","a":"D","why":"D. The arrow points to a small duct lying <b>within the pale connective-tissue septum between lobules</b>: an <b>interlobular duct</b>, lined by cuboidal to columnar cells.<br>A. There is no 'centroacinar duct'; centroacinar cells sit inside the acini.<br>B. Intralobular ducts lie inside the lobule, among the acini.<br>C. The pancreas has no striated ducts.<br>E. Intercalated ducts are tiny tubes of flat cells inside the lobule, leaving each acinus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G116-54.jpg","in":"Same pancreas field as Q7; black arrow on a small duct lined by low cuboidal cells inside the pale connective-tissue septum. Asked: the arrowed structure.","tg":["H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)","H16 slide 12 (Ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":54,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":9,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":95,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":196,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1,"n":"40"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":19,"label":"Serous demilune: serous cap on a mucous acinus","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G020-19.jpg","cap":"Serous demilunes (S) capping mucous acini (M); mostly sublingual, also submandibular","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 19","lg":[["A","serous acinus"],["S","serous demilunes (arrows; dark caps on mucous tubules)"],["M","mucous cells (pale)"],["ID","small intralobular duct (arrow)"]]},{"src":"../sl/G020-72.jpg","cap":"Arrows on dark serous crescents capping pale mucous tubules","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 72"}],"q":[{"id":"G024-9-1","s":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the structure indicated by the arrow?","o":["A. The organ shown in the image is the parotid gland","B. The structure squeezes the secretion from the acini","C. The structure secretes trypsinogen","D. Contributes a serous component to the gland's seromucous secretion","E. It has basal infoldings"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Contributes a serous component to the gland's seromucous secretion","a":"D","why":"D. The arrow marks a <b>serous demilune</b>: a dark crescent of serous cells capping a pale mucous tubule. It adds the <b>serous (watery, protein) part</b> of the gland's mixed seromucous saliva.<br>A. Pale mucous tubules mean a mixed gland; the parotid is purely serous.<br>B. Squeezing secretion out is the myoepithelial cell's job.<br>C. Trypsinogen is made by pancreatic acinar cells.<br>E. Basal infoldings belong to striated ducts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G115-8.jpg","in":"Mixed salivary gland, H&E: mostly purple serous acini with a central cluster of pale, foamy mucous acini; the black arrow points to a dark purple crescent of serous cells capping a mucous acinus (serous demilune). Same micrograph as G024 slide 9. On the slide the picture covers the ends of options A and B.","tg":["H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)","H16 slide 11 (Myoepithelial cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2026","sl":8,"f":"G115"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":9,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":9,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":12,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":9,"f":"G025"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":7,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":1,"n":"41"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":20,"label":"Parotid: all-serous acini, fat cells, striated ducts","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G020-75.jpg","cap":"Parotid: dark serous acini separated by many empty fat cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 75"},{"src":"../sl/G020-15.jpg","cap":"Parotid: all serous acini with intercalated and striated ducts, no mucous cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 15","lg":[["a","parotid, plastic section, toluidine blue-based stain"],["SD","striated duct"],["A","serous acini"],["ID","intercalated duct"],["b","parotid, hematoxylin and eosin"],["CT","connective-tissue septum"],["lines at left edge of the atlas plate (labels cut off), top to bottom","venule; interlobular excretory duct; fat cells; interlobular excretory duct; serous acini; connective-tissue septa between lobules; myoepithelial cells (on inset I)"],["I","serous acinus"],["II","intercalated duct"],["III","striated duct"],["IV","interlobular excretory duct"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3959-28-1","s":"D6- [Identify the gland]","o":["A. Submandibular glands","B. Parotid gland","C. Sublingual gland"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid gland","a":"B","why":"B. A lobulated gland of <b>uniformly dark purple serous acini</b>, with ducts in the septa and <b>no pale mucous areas</b>, is the <b>parotid</b>.<br>A. Submandibular is mixed: pale mucous units would be scattered among the dark acini.<br>C. Sublingual is mostly mucous, so it would look pale.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G116-15.jpg","in":"Low-power whole section of a lobulated gland: uniformly dark purple serous acini, septa with large ducts and vessels, no pale mucous areas. Asked: which salivary gland (A-C).","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":15,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":28,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":25,"f":"G022"}],"tier":1,"n":"42"},{"id":"F0052-192-1","s":"Question 6 Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Spleen","B. Pancreas","C. Parotid gland","D. Sublingual gland","E. Liver"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid gland","a":"C","why":"C. Clusters of <b>dark granular serous acini</b> separated by <b>large empty fat cells</b>, with no mucous cells and no islets, mean the <b>parotid</b>.<br>A. Spleen is lymphoid tissue with red and white pulp.<br>B. Pancreas is tightly packed acini with pale islets and little fat.<br>D. Sublingual is mostly pale mucous tubules.<br>E. Liver is plates of hepatocytes with sinusoids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G116-52.jpg","in":"Clusters of dark granular serous acini separated by large clear fat cells, with a large pink striated duct; no mucous cells, no islets. Asked: which organ.","tg":["H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)","H16 slide 30 (Compare)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":52,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":192,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1,"n":"43"},{"id":"F3959-31-1","s":"D9- [Identify the gland]","o":["A. Submandibular glands","B. Parotid gland","C. Sublingual gland"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid gland","a":"B","why":"B. Only <b>dark serous acini</b>, with <b>many fat cells</b> and a duct in connective tissue, mean the <b>parotid</b>. It is purely serous, and fat cells among its acini are typical.<br>A. Submandibular would show pale mucous units among the serous acini.<br>C. Sublingual is mostly pale mucous tubules.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G116-17.jpg","in":"Two views of one gland. Left: dark serous acini scattered among many clear fat cells, beside dense connective tissue with a duct. Right: closer view, dark granular serous acini, fat cells and a pink striated duct. Asked: which salivary gland (A-C).","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":17,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":31,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":28,"f":"G022"}],"tier":1,"n":"44"},{"id":"F0052-188-1","s":"Question 4 Identify the organ:","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Pancreas","C. Parotid gland","D. Stomach","E. Liver"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid gland","a":"C","why":"C. The picture is one <b>striated duct</b> (tall pink columnar cells with basal striations) among dark <b>serous acini</b>. Striated ducts plus serous acini mean a serous salivary gland: the <b>parotid</b>.<br>A. Duodenum shows villi, crypts and Brunner glands.<br>B. The pancreas has serous acini but <b>no striated ducts</b>.<br>D. Stomach shows pits and gastric glands.<br>E. Liver shows hepatocyte plates and sinusoids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/F0052-188.jpg","in":"Same high-power image: a striated duct (columnar cells with basal striations) surrounded by granular serous acini.","tg":["H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)","H16 slide 30 (Compare)","H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":50,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":188,"f":"F0052"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":5,"f":"F3959"}],"tier":1,"n":"45"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":21,"label":"Submandibular: mixed, mostly serous","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G020-17.jpg","cap":"Submandibular atlas plate: mostly dark serous acini with some pale mucous acini","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 17"},{"src":"../sl/G020-71.jpg","cap":"Submandibular at low power: mostly dark serous lobules with pale mucous patches","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 71"}],"q":[{"id":"F3959-29-1","s":"D7- [Identify the gland]","o":["A. Submandibular glands","B. Parotid gland","C. Sublingual gland"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Submandibular gland","a":"A","why":"A. <b>Mostly dark serous acini</b> with a <b>minority of pale mucous tubules</b> and pink striated ducts: a mixed, mostly serous gland is the <b>submandibular</b>.<br>B. Parotid is purely serous; it has no pale mucous tubules.<br>C. Sublingual is mostly mucous: pale tubules would dominate, and striated ducts are few.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G116-16.jpg","in":"Medium power: mostly dark granular serous acini, a minority of pale foamy mucous tubules with dark demilune caps, and several pink striated ducts. Asked: which salivary gland (A-C).","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 17 (Submandibular gland)","H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":16,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":29,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":26,"f":"G022"}],"tier":1,"n":"46"},{"id":"F0052-184-1","s":"Question 2 Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Submandibular gland","B. Spleen","C. Parotid gland","D. Sublingual gland","E. Liver"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Submandibular gland","a":"A","why":"A. <b>Dark serous acini mixed with pale mucous tubules</b>, some capped by serous demilunes, plus several striated ducts: a mixed, mostly serous gland is the <b>submandibular</b>.<br>B. Spleen is lymphoid tissue, not acini.<br>C. Parotid is purely serous: no pale mucous tubules.<br>D. Sublingual is mostly mucous: pale tubules would dominate.<br>E. Liver is plates of hepatocytes with sinusoids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G116-48.jpg","in":"Dark purple serous acini mixed with pale foamy mucous units capped by dark crescents (demilunes), plus pink striated ducts. Asked: which organ.","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 17 (Submandibular gland)","H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":48,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":184,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1,"n":"47"},{"id":"G118-17-1","s":"Identify the organ presented in the image below. [The letters printed on the picture (SA, MA, SD, StD, ID, A) label structures; they are not the answer choices.]","o":["A. Parotid gland","B. Sublingual gland","C. Submandibular gland","D. Exocrine pancreas"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Submandibular gland","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Submandibular gland.</b> Two kinds of secretory units lie side by side: dark, granular <b>serous acini</b> (SA) and pale <b>mucous acini</b> (MA), some capped by serous demilunes (SD). A mixed gland that still has many whole serous acini, plus a striated duct (StD), is the submandibular.<br>A. Parotid gland: only serous acini; no pale mucous cells and no demilunes.<br>B. Sublingual gland: also mixed, but almost every unit is a pale mucous tubule; whole serous acini are rare.<br>D. Exocrine pancreas: only serous acini, with no mucous cells and no striated ducts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G118-17.jpg","in":"Mixed salivary gland, H&E, with the textbook's letter labels left on the picture: SA = dark serous acini, MA = pale mucous acini (lumens marked *), SD = serous demilunes, ID = intercalated duct, StD = striated duct, A = fat cell. Serous and mucous acini side by side, with many serous acini.","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 17 (Submandibular gland)","H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":17,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"48"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":21,"label":"Sublingual: mostly pale mucous acini","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G020-73.jpg","cap":"Sublingual gland: field of pale bluish mucous acini, almost no serous cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 73"},{"src":"../sl/G020-9.jpg","cap":"Mucous acini (M) with flat basal nuclei beside skeletal muscle (SM)","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 9","lg":[["M","mucous cells in tubules (pale, foamy)"],["ID","small intralobular ducts (arrows)"],["SM","skeletal muscle bundles of the tongue"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3959-2-1","s":"Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Submandibular gland","B. Spleen","C. Parotid gland","D. Sublingual gland","E. Liver"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Sublingual gland","a":"D","why":"D. Under a capsule, the gland is almost all <b>pale bluish-gray mucous tubules</b>, with very few dark serous cells. A mostly mucous gland is the <b>sublingual</b>.<br>A. Submandibular is mixed but mostly serous: dark acini would dominate.<br>B. Spleen is lymphoid tissue (white and red pulp), not glandular acini.<br>C. Parotid is purely serous: dark granular acini, no mucous cells.<br>E. Liver is plates of hepatocytes with sinusoids, not acini.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G116-47.jpg","in":"Capsule over a gland made almost entirely of pale bluish-grey mucous tubules with very few dark serous cells. Asked: which organ.","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 16 (Sublingual gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":47,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":2,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":3,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":87,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":182,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1,"n":"49"},{"id":"F3959-26-1","s":"D4- [Identify the gland]","o":["A. Submandibular glands","B. Parotid gland","C. Sublingual gland"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Sublingual gland","a":"C","why":"C. Pale, bluish <b>mucous tubules fill the whole field</b>, with almost no dark serous cells. A gland that is mostly mucous is the <b>sublingual</b>.<br>A. Submandibular is mostly serous: dark purple acini would dominate, with scattered pale units.<br>B. Parotid is purely serous: uniformly dark granular acini, often with fat cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G116-14.jpg","in":"Two views of one gland. Left: low-power strip of a lobulated, pale bluish gland under a thin capsule. Right: closer view, pale foamy mucous tubules almost throughout with a few small ducts. Asked: which salivary gland (A-C).","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 16 (Sublingual gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":14,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":26,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":23,"f":"G022"}],"tier":1,"n":"50"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":22,"label":"Identify pancreas: packed acini with islets","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G020-77.jpg","cap":"Low-power pancreas: pale round islets scattered among packed acini","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 77"},{"src":"../sl/G020-28.jpg","cap":"Labelled pancreas: acini, islets, septa with interlobular duct, centroacinar cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"F3959-6-1","s":"Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Spleen","B. Pancreas","C. Parotid gland","D. Sublingual gland","E. Liver"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pancreas","a":"B","why":"B. Densely packed serous acini with <b>pale, round clusters of cells (islets of Langerhans)</b> mean <b>pancreas</b>. No other organ has islets.<br>A. Spleen is lymphoid tissue, with no acini.<br>C. Parotid has serous acini but no islets; look for fat cells and striated ducts.<br>D. Sublingual is mostly pale mucous tubules.<br>E. Liver is plates of hepatocytes with sinusoids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G116-51.jpg","in":"Densely packed serous acini with pale, rounded clusters of lightly stained cells (islets) at the left and centre. Asked: which organ.","tg":["H16 slide 24 (Mixed gland)","H16 slide 25 (Capsule, Septa, lobules)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":51,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":6,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":7,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":8,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":91,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":190,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1,"n":"51"},{"id":"F0052-194-1","s":"Question 7 Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Spleen","B. Pancreas","C. Parotid gland","D. Sublingual gland","E. Liver"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pancreas","a":"B","why":"B. A uniform field of <b>tightly packed serous acini</b> with <b>pink apical zymogen granules</b>, thin septa and a duct in connective tissue, but <b>no fat cells, mucous cells or striated ducts</b>: the <b>pancreas</b>.<br>A. Spleen is lymphoid tissue without acini.<br>C. Parotid would show fat cells and pink striated ducts among its acini.<br>D. Sublingual is mostly pale mucous tubules.<br>E. Liver is plates of hepatocytes with sinusoids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G116-53.jpg","in":"Uniform field of serous acini with basophilic bases and pink apical granules; a pale connective-tissue septum with a small duct at the lower right; no fat cells or mucous cells. Asked: which organ.","tg":["H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)","H16 slide 30 (Compare)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":53,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":194,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1,"n":"52"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":23,"label":"Centroacinar cell: pale nucleus in acinus center","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G020-26.jpg","cap":"Pancreas micrograph with arrow on centroacinar nucleus; acinus diagram with centroacinar cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 26","lg":[["A","pancreatic acini"],["F","fibroblast (arrow)"],["arrow without a letter","centroacinar cell (pale nucleus inside the acinus)"]]},{"src":"../sl/G024-24.jpg","cap":"Acinus diagram and micrograph: centroacinar cells begin the intercalated duct inside the acinus","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 24","lg":[["A","pancreatic acini (serous)"],["F","fibroblast in the thin connective tissue (letter shows backwards: micrograph is mirror-flipped)"],["circle","one acinus cut through its centre"],["arrow","centroacinar cell (pale nucleus in the acinus centre, start of the intercalated duct)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3959-10-1","s":"Which structure is shown by the arrow?","o":["A. Centroacinar cell","B. Serous cell","C. Striated duct","D. Intercalated duct"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Centroacinar cell","a":"A","why":"A. The arrow is on a <b>small pale nucleus in the center of an acinus</b>: a <b>centroacinar cell</b>, the first cell of the intercalated duct. The nearby islet confirms pancreas.<br>B. Serous (acinar) cells are the dark pyramidal cells with pink apical granules around the lumen.<br>C. The pancreas has no striated ducts.<br>D. The intercalated duct is the tube leaving the acinus; inside the acinus its cells are called centroacinar cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G116-55.jpg","in":"Pancreas with an islet at the top; white arrow on a small pale nucleus sitting in the centre of an acinus. Asked: the arrowed structure.","tg":["H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)","H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":55,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":10,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":10,"f":"G022"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":198,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1,"n":"53"},{"id":"G024-21-1","s":"A biopsy is performed and the specimen below is obtained. Which of the following is true of the organ from which the specimen was taken?","o":["A. The apical cytoplasm of acinar cells contains prominent RER and Golgi","B. The basal cytoplasm of acinar cells contains prominent zymogen granules","C. Centroacinar cells are continuous with intercalated ducts","D. Serous demilunes are usually present","E. Striated ducts are present"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Centroacinar cells are continuous with intercalated ducts","a":"C","why":"C. The specimen is <b>exocrine pancreas</b>, with pale centroacinar nuclei in the acinar centers. <b>Centroacinar cells</b> are the first cells of the <b>intercalated duct</b>, so they are continuous with it.<br>A. Rough endoplasmic reticulum is basal; that is why the base stains purple.<br>B. Zymogen granules are apical (pink), not basal.<br>D. Serous demilunes belong to mixed salivary glands.<br>E. The pancreas has no striated ducts; they belong to salivary glands.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G115-17.jpg","in":"Exocrine pancreas, H&E, high power: serous acinar cells with dark basal nuclei and purple bases, pink granular (zymogen) tops, and small pale nuclei in the centres of acini (centroacinar cells); no striated ducts, no demilunes. Same micrograph as G024 slide 21.","tg":["H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)","H16 slide 30 (Compare)","H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2026","sl":17,"f":"G115"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":21,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":21,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":27,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":21,"f":"G025"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":23,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":1,"n":"54"},{"id":"G118-13-1","s":"Which description best applies to the cell at the tip of the arrow labeled A?","o":["A. Is part of an intercalated duct","B. Only found in the parotid","C. Secretes digestive enzymes","D. Is abnormally pale-staining"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Is part of an intercalated duct","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Is part of an intercalated duct.</b> Arrow A is on a small <b>pale nucleus in the center of a pancreatic acinus</b>: a <b>centroacinar cell</b>. Centroacinar cells are the first cells of the intercalated duct, which begins inside the acinus.<br>B. Only found in the parotid: the reverse is true; centroacinar cells are found only in the pancreas.<br>C. Secretes digestive enzymes: that is the job of the acinar cells around it, full of pink zymogen granules. Centroacinar cells have no granules; they secrete a bicarbonate-rich fluid.<br>D. Is abnormally pale-staining: pale staining is its normal look.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G118-13.jpg","in":"Exocrine pancreas, H&E: serous acini full of pink zymogen granules (a bracket marks one acinus); the arrow labeled A points to a small pale nucleus in the centre of an acinus (centroacinar cell); three more unlabeled arrows at the right edge point to pale strips between acini.","tg":["H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)","H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":13,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"55"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":24,"label":"Pancreas vs parotid: islets, centroacinar cells, striated ducts","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G020-30.jpg","cap":"Compare table: islets, centroacinar cells in pancreas; myoepithelial cells, striated ducts in salivary","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 30"},{"src":"../sl/F0754-215.jpg","cap":"Parotid striated duct with no centroacinar cells versus pancreas with centroacinar cells","where":"2022 TA review, slide 215","lg":[["SD","striated duct"],["A","serous acini"],["left yellow arrow","striated duct (parotid)"],["F","fibroblast (arrow)"],["right yellow arrow","centroacinar cell (pancreas)"]]},{"src":"../sl/G020-75.jpg","cap":"Parotid: dark serous acini separated by many empty fat cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 75"}],"q":[{"id":"G118-14-1","s":"Identify the organ presented in the image below.","o":["A. Sublingual gland","B. Submandibular gland","C. Exocrine pancreas","D. Parotid gland"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Exocrine pancreas","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Exocrine pancreas.</b> Every secretory unit is a serous acinus packed with pink zymogen granules, with no fat cells between the acini. The ducts decide it: the thin duct outlined at A (an intercalated duct) starts among the acinar cells, and the larger ducts at B are lined by <b>pale cells, not the bright pink, striped cells of a striated duct</b>. A serous gland with <b>no striated ducts</b> is the pancreas; its ducts begin inside the acinus as pale centroacinar cells.<br>A. Sublingual gland: mostly pale mucous tubules.<br>B. Submandibular gland: mixed, with pale mucous acini and serous demilunes.<br>D. Parotid gland: also all serous, but it has bright pink striated ducts and many fat cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G118-14.jpg","in":"Gland made only of serous acini packed with dark pink granules; no mucous acini. Label A (its name is covered) points to a thin pale duct, outlined in black, that leads out of an acinus (intercalated duct); label B (name covered) points to two larger pale ducts with a small lumen.","tg":["H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)","H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)","H16 slide 30 (Compare)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":14,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"56"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":25,"label":"Portal venule on a slide: oxygen-poor, nutrient-rich blood","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G020-45.jpg","cap":"Portal triad: large thin-walled portal vein branch, small hepatic artery, cuboidal bile duct","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 45"},{"src":"../sl/G116-39.jpg","cap":"Portal venule blood (nutrient-rich, oxygen-poor) and arteriole blood (oxygen-rich) mix in the sinusoid","where":"2026 lab, slide 39"}],"q":[{"id":"G118-15-1","s":"Which structure brings poor O2/rich nutrient blood into this organ?","o":["A. 1","B. 2","C. 3","D. 4"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"1","a":"A","why":"<b>A. 1, the portal venule.</b> Blood that is <b>poor in oxygen but rich in nutrients</b> comes from the gut through the <b>portal vein</b>. In a portal triad its branch is the <b>largest vessel, with a thin wall</b> and a wide lumen: structure 1.<br>B. 2 is the bile ductule: a ring of tall cuboidal epithelial cells. It carries bile out, not blood in.<br>C. 3 is the hepatic arteriole: small and round, with a thick muscular wall. It brings oxygen-rich blood.<br>D. 4 marks the hepatocytes, the liver cells that receive the blood.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G118-15.jpg","in":"Liver portal triad, H&E: 1 = the largest vessel, thin-walled with a wide lumen (portal venule); 2 = duct lined by tall cuboidal epithelium (bile ductule); 3 = small round vessel with a thick muscular wall (hepatic arteriole); 4 = the hepatocytes beside the triad.","tg":["H16 slide 40 (Dual blood supply of liver)","H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)","H16 slide 43 (LIVER - HISTOLOGY)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":15,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"57"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":26,"label":"Portal triad micrograph: venule, arteriole, ductule","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G020-45.jpg","cap":"Portal triad: large portal vein branch, small artery, cuboidal bile duct","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 45"},{"src":"../sl/G020-44.jpg","cap":"Venule (V), arteriole (A), ductule (D) at lobule corner; central vein (C) alone","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 44","lg":[["C","central vein"],["A","hepatic arterioles"],["V","portal venule"],["D","bile ductule"],["arrows","thin connective-tissue septa outlining the lobule (pig liver)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-200-1","s":"Question 10 Identify the structures labeled as 1, 2 and 3 respectively:","o":["A. Bile ductule, venule, arteriole","B. Bile ductule, arteriole, venule","C. Arteriole, capillary, venule","D. Arteriole, venule, capillary"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Bile ductule, arteriole, venule","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Bile ductule, arteriole, venule.</b><br>1 = <b>bile ductule</b>: a ring of <b>cuboidal epithelial cells</b> around a small lumen.<br>2 = <b>hepatic arteriole</b>: the small vessel with a thick wall and narrow lumen.<br>3 = <b>portal venule</b>: the largest, thin-walled lumen holding red cells.<br>A. Swaps the arteriole and the venule.<br>C and D. No capillary is labelled; capillaries are not part of the triad (the liver's capillary-like channels are the sinusoids inside the lobule).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/F0052-200.jpg","in":"H&E portal area: #1 a small duct lined by cuboidal cells, #2 a small thick-walled vessel, #3 a large thin-walled vessel containing red cells; curved arrow on the limiting plate.","tg":["H16 slide 43 (LIVER - HISTOLOGY)","H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":56,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":200,"f":"F0052"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":11,"f":"F3959"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["1","bile ductule (ring of cuboidal cells)"],["2","arteriole (hepatic artery branch: small, thick wall)"],["3","portal venule (portal vein branch: biggest, thin wall)"],["curved arrow","limiting plate (first row of hepatocytes around the portal tract)"]],"n":"58"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":27,"label":"Hepatocyte polarity: apical canalicular, basal sinusoidal","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G020-48.jpg","cap":"Hepatocyte surfaces: sinusoidal (basal) and canalicular (apical) domains, tight junctions","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 48"},{"src":"../sl/G021-35.jpg","cap":"Hepatocyte diagram: apical bile canaliculus; basolateral side facing perisinusoidal space","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 35","lg":[["1","plasma proteins made and secreted into the perisinusoidal space"],["2","bilirubin, bile acids, toxins taken from blood and sent into the bile canaliculus"],["RER","rough endoplasmic reticulum"],["SER","smooth endoplasmic reticulum"],["3","glucose taken up and stored as glycogen, released when needed"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G116-28-1","s":"Is the hepatocyte a polarized cell?","o":["A. Yes: the apical domain faces the sinusoid and the basolateral domain faces the bile canaliculus","B. Yes: the apical domain faces the bile canaliculus and the basolateral domain faces the sinusoid","C. Yes: the apical domain faces the central vein and the basolateral domain faces the portal triad","D. No: all of its surfaces are alike, because it does not line a duct or the gut lumen"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Yes: the apical domain faces the bile canaliculus and the basolateral domain faces the sinusoid","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Yes: apical toward the bile canaliculus, basolateral toward the sinusoid.</b> The hepatocyte secretes <b>bile from its apical (canalicular) surface</b>, a groove sealed by tight junctions. Its <b>basolateral (sinusoidal) surface</b>, covered with microvilli, faces the space of Disse and exchanges material with the blood.<br>A. Reverses the two domains; bile would then be released toward the blood.<br>C. The central vein and the portal triad are the two ends of the lobule, not two surfaces of one cell.<br>D. A cell does not have to line a duct to be polarized; the hepatocyte's surfaces differ in structure and in what they release.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Line drawing of one hepatocyte: sinusoids and the space of Disse (with microvilli) at the top and the lower corners, green bile canaliculi between neighbouring hepatocytes at the sides; slide 29 adds the labels 'Apical' (at the canaliculi) and 'Basolateral' (at the sinusoidal surfaces). Not needed to answer.","tg":["H16 slide 48 (HEPATOCYTES)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":28,"f":"G116"}],"tier":1,"n":"59"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":28,"label":"Hepatic sinusoid on a slide: blood channel between hepatocyte plates","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G115-10.jpg","cap":"Hepatic sinusoids: pale blood channels between plates of hepatocytes one or two cells thick","where":"2026 interactive, slide 10"},{"src":"../sl/G116-33.jpg","cap":"Hepatocyte plates and sinusoids on H&E; reticulin stain outlines them in black","where":"2026 lab, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"G118-11-1","s":"Identify the structure at the tip of the pointer","o":["A. Lamina propria","B. Red pulp","C. Hepatic sinusoid","D. Fenestrated capillary"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic sinusoid","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Hepatic sinusoid.</b> The field is liver: plates of large pink hepatocytes with round central nuclei. The pointer is in a <b>pale channel between two plates that holds red blood cells</b>, which is a sinusoid.<br>A. Lamina propria: loose connective tissue under a lining epithelium (gut, gallbladder); there is none here.<br>B. Red pulp: part of the spleen, which has no plates of hepatocytes.<br>D. Fenestrated capillary: a narrow capillary whose pores sit on a continuous basal lamina (kidney, gut, endocrine glands). The liver's channel is a wider sinusoid with gaps between its cells and no continuous basal lamina.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G118-11.jpg","in":"Liver, H&E: plates of pink hepatocytes with round nuclei; the arrow points into a pale channel between the plates that holds red blood cells (hepatic sinusoid).","tg":["H16 slide 41 (LIVER)","H16 slide 50 (OTHER CELLS)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":11,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"60"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":28,"label":"Space of Disse: blood-hepatocyte exchange","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G020-53.jpg","cap":"Electron micrograph: fenestrated endothelium; space of Disse full of hepatocyte microvilli","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 53","lg":[["sER","smooth endoplasmic reticulum"],["rER","rough endoplasmic reticulum"],["red circle","hepatic artery branch"],["green outline","portal vein branch"],["cyan circle","bile duct"],["blue double line","limiting plate ('mura': ring of hepatocytes around the portal canal)"],["11","slide number of the source set, not a structure"]]},{"src":"../sl/G020-48.jpg","cap":"Diagram: fenestrated endothelium, space of Disse between sinusoid and hepatocytes","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 48"}],"q":[{"id":"G116-35-1","s":"An electron micrograph of the liver shows a sinusoid. A narrow space packed with hepatocyte microvilli lies between the thin, fenestrated wall of the sinusoid and the hepatocyte. What is this space?","o":["A. Space of Mall (periportal space)","B. Bile canaliculus","C. Space of Disse (perisinusoidal space)","D. Lumen of the sinusoid","E. Canal of Hering"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Space of Disse (perisinusoidal space)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Space of Disse (perisinusoidal space).</b> It lies <b>between the fenestrated endothelium and the hepatocyte</b> and is filled with <b>hepatocyte microvilli</b>. Plasma enters it through the pores, so exchange happens here.<br>A. Space of Mall: at the edge of the lobule, between the portal connective tissue and the limiting plate; lymph begins there.<br>B. Bile canaliculus: also holds microvilli, but it lies between two hepatocytes, sealed by tight junctions, away from the sinusoid.<br>D. Lumen of the sinusoid: inside the endothelium; it holds the blood cells.<br>E. Canal of Hering: a small bile channel near the portal triad, lined partly by cuboidal cholangiocytes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Only a labeled version exists (electron micrograph with the labels Lumen of sinusoid, Fenestrae, Endothelial cell, Space of Disse, Microvilli on hepatocyte, Mitochondria, sER, rER), so the view is described in the stem.","tg":["H16 slide 53 (SPACES)","H16 slide 50 (OTHER CELLS)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":35,"f":"G116"}],"tier":1,"n":"61"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":28,"label":"Sinusoids: endothelium + Kupffer cells, drain centrally","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G020-47.jpg","cap":"Sinusoid from portal field to central vein: endothelium, Kupffer cell, space of Disse","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 47","lg":[["arrows","flow: blood (blue venous, red arterial) along the sinusoid into the central vein; bile (green) the other way, to the bile ductule"]]},{"src":"../sl/G024-14.jpg","cap":"Electron micrographs: endothelial cells line the sinusoid; Kupffer cell inside it","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 14","lg":[["sER","smooth endoplasmic reticulum"],["rER","rough endoplasmic reticulum"]]},{"src":"../sl/G024-16.jpg","cap":"Hepatocyte plates one to two cells thick between sinusoids","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"G024-12-1","s":"Which of the following is true of the structures identified by the 2 thin arrows and the arrowhead?","o":["A. The structures marked by the arrows and the structure marked by the arrowhead are not continuous","B. The lumen marked by the arrows is associated with both endothelial cells and Kupffer cells","C. The structure marked by the arrowhead is within zone 1 of the hepatic acinus","D. The structure marked by the arrowhead contains 1-2 layers of smooth muscle in its wall","E. The structures marked by the arrows are apposed to plates of hepatocytes that are usually 3-4 layers thick"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The lumen marked by the arrows is associated with both endothelial cells and Kupffer cells","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The arrows mark <b>sinusoids</b>, lined by <b>fenestrated endothelial cells</b> with <b>Kupffer cells</b> (macrophages) in the lining. The arrowhead is the <b>central vein</b>.<br>A. Sinusoids <b>are</b> continuous with the central vein; they drain into it.<br>C. The central vein is in <b>zone 3</b>; zone 1 is next to the portal triad.<br>D. The central vein has only a thin connective tissue wall, no smooth muscle layers.<br>E. Hepatocyte plates are <b>one to two cells</b> thick.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G115-9.jpg","in":"Liver, H&E (PathPedia): a black arrowhead inside a thin-walled central vein; two thin black arrows on sinusoids that run between the hepatocyte plates toward it. Same micrograph as G024 slide 12.","tg":["H16 slide 50 (OTHER CELLS)","H16 slide 51 (Kupffer cells)","H16 slide 42 (Hepatic lobule)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2026","sl":9,"f":"G115"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":12,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":12,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":16,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":12,"f":"G025"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":13,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":1,"n":"62"},{"id":"G116-35-2","s":"An electron micrograph of the liver shows a sinusoid. A very thin cell with open pores (fenestrae) separates the lumen of the sinusoid from the microvillus-filled perisinusoidal space. Which cell is it?","o":["A. Kupffer cell","B. Hepatic stellate (Ito) cell","C. Hepatocyte","D. Sinusoidal endothelial cell","E. Cholangiocyte"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Sinusoidal endothelial cell","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Sinusoidal endothelial cell.</b> The wall of the sinusoid is a <b>very thin endothelial cell with open pores (fenestrae)</b> and no continuous basal lamina. It is the only layer between the lumen and the space of Disse.<br>A. Kupffer cell: a bulky macrophage that bulges into the lumen and holds engulfed material.<br>B. Hepatic stellate (Ito) cell: lies outside the endothelium, in the space of Disse, and holds lipid droplets.<br>C. Hepatocyte: the large cell beyond the space; it sends the microvilli into it.<br>E. Cholangiocyte: the cuboidal cell that lines bile ductules, not sinusoids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Only a labeled version exists (electron micrograph with the labels Lumen of sinusoid, Fenestrae, Endothelial cell, Space of Disse, Microvilli on hepatocyte, Mitochondria, sER, rER), so the view is described in the stem.","tg":["H16 slide 50 (OTHER CELLS)","H16 slide 53 (SPACES)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":35,"f":"G116"}],"tier":1,"n":"63"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":29,"label":"Kupffer cells: sinusoidal macrophages, antigen-presenting","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G020-51.jpg","cap":"India ink: black Kupffer cells (K) along sinusoids; macrophage functions listed","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 51","lg":[["K","Kupffer cells (black from injected India ink)"]]},{"src":"../sl/G024-14.jpg","cap":"Electron micrograph: Kupffer cell (macrophage) inside the sinusoid lumen","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 14","lg":[["sER","smooth endoplasmic reticulum"],["rER","rough endoplasmic reticulum"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G118-1-1","s":"What is the function of the cell at the tip of the arrow?","o":["A. Phagocytosis","B. Replenishing stem cell","C. Fat storage","D. Bile production"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Phagocytosis","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Phagocytosis.</b> The arrowed cell lies <b>in the lumen of a sinusoid</b>, between the hepatocytes, and its cytoplasm is <b>full of engulfed brown-yellow granules</b>. It is a <b>Kupffer cell</b>, the liver's macrophage, which removes aged red blood cells, bacteria and debris.<br>B. Replenishing stem cell: the liver's progenitor cells lie in the canals of Hering, not in the sinusoid.<br>C. Fat storage: the job of the hepatic stellate (Ito) cell, which lies in the space of Disse and holds clear lipid droplets.<br>D. Bile production: the job of the hepatocytes, the large pink cells with round nuclei around it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G118-1.jpg","in":"Liver, H&E, high power: pink hepatocytes with round nuclei around a sinusoid; the arrow points to a cell lying in the sinusoid whose cytoplasm is full of engulfed brown-yellow granules (Kupffer cell).","tg":["H16 slide 51 (Kupffer cells)","H16 slide 50 (OTHER CELLS)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":1,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"64"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":30,"label":"Ito cells: vitamin A, fat, fibrosis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G020-52.jpg","cap":"Ito cell (HS) with lipid droplets in the space of Disse; functions listed","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 52","lg":[["HS","hepatic stellate (Ito) cell with lipid droplets"],["K","Kupffer cells"],["PS","perisinusoidal space of Disse (arrow)"],["E","endothelial cell"],["H","hepatocytes"],["S","sinusoid"]]},{"src":"../sl/G024-7.jpg","cap":"Electron micrograph: hepatic stellate cell in the perisinusoidal space of Disse","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 7","lg":[["HSC","hepatic stellate (Ito) cell, packed with pale lipid droplets (vitamin A store)"],["arrow","perisinusoidal space of Disse (gap between the sinusoid's endothelial lining and the hepatocytes, where the stellate cell sits)"]]},{"src":"../sl/G024-4.jpg","cap":"Trichrome: spider-web pericellular fibrosis laid down by activated stellate cells","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"G024-5-1","s":"[Case context, slides 2-4: 45-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome and central obesity; fatigue, dull RUQ pain; transaminases twice the upper limit of normal; ultrasound shows fatty liver (NAFLD); biopsy shows steatosis, inflammation and 'spider web like' pericellular fibrosis on trichrome = NASH.] An activated hepatic stellate cell with a myofibroblast-like phenotype has been implicated as a primary effector cell in hepatic fibrogenesis. Given this information and the \"spider web like\" histologic appearance of fibrosis in this patient's biopsy, The most likely location of this early fibrosis is:","o":["A. Portal triads","B. Canals of Hering","C. Perisinusoidal space of Disse","D. Hepatocyte cytoplasm"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Perisinusoidal space of Disse","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Perisinusoidal space of Disse.</b> Activated <b>hepatic stellate (Ito) cells</b> live there; after injury they become myofibroblast-like and lay collagen around single hepatocytes, giving the 'spider web' (pericellular) pattern.<br>A. Portal triad fibrosis is a portal pattern (chronic viral hepatitis, biliary disease), not pericellular.<br>B. Canals of Hering are small bile channels near the triad, lined by cholangiocytes.<br>D. Matrix is laid down outside cells, not in hepatocyte cytoplasm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)","H16 slide 53 (SPACES)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2026","sl":4,"f":"G115"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":5,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":5,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":5,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":5,"f":"G025"}],"tier":1,"n":"65"},{"id":"G118-9-1","s":"Identify the cells at the tip of the pointers.","o":["A. Stave cells","B. Kupfer cells","C. Thymic epithelioreticular cells","D. Stellate cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Stellate cells","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Stellate cells.</b> Each pointer is on a cell that holds a <b>large, clear, round fat droplet</b>, with a small dark nucleus pushed to its edge, lying <b>beside a sinusoid</b> between the hepatocytes. The fat-storing cells there are the hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, which also store vitamin A.<br>A. Stave cells: the long endothelial cells that line the venous sinuses of the spleen.<br>B. Kupffer cells: macrophages inside the sinusoid lumen; they hold engulfed debris, not one clear fat droplet.<br>C. Thymic epithelioreticular cells: the supporting framework cells of the thymus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G118-9.jpg","in":"Liver, H&E, high power (scale bar 20 µm): four white arrows on cells that hold one large, clear, round fat droplet with a small dark nucleus pushed to its edge, lying beside the sinusoids between the hepatocytes (fat-storing stellate cells, also called Ito cells).","tg":["H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)","H16 slide 50 (OTHER CELLS)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":9,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"66"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":30,"label":"Fatty liver: ultrasound finds fat, biopsy confirms steatohepatitis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G024-4.jpg","cap":"Steatohepatitis biopsy: fat droplets, inflammatory clusters, spider-web trichrome fibrosis","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"G115-2-1","s":"A 45-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus, metabolic syndrome and abdominal (central) obesity feels more tired than usual and has mild dull pain in the right upper quadrant. His serum transaminase levels, previously normal, are now twice the upper limit of normal. His physician orders an abdominal ultrasound examination. What can the ultrasound detect in his liver?","o":["A. Activated hepatic stellate cells in the space of Disse","B. Fat in the liver (steatosis), seen as increased echogenicity","C. Steatohepatitis (inflammation and injury of hepatocytes)","D. \"Spider web like\" fibrosis around the hepatocytes"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Fat in the liver (steatosis), seen as increased echogenicity","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Fat in the liver (steatosis), seen as increased echogenicity.</b> A fatty liver reflects more sound, so it looks <b>brighter</b> on ultrasound. With type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome, central obesity and newly raised transaminases (liver enzymes), fat in the liver is the likely cause.<br>A. Activated stellate cells are single cells in the space of Disse; only a microscope shows them.<br>C. Steatohepatitis (inflamed, injured hepatocytes) cannot be told from simple fat on ultrasound; that takes a biopsy.<br>D. Fibrosis around single hepatocytes shows on a biopsy stained with trichrome, not on ultrasound.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2026","sl":2,"f":"G115"}],"tier":1,"n":"67"},{"id":"G115-3-1","s":"A man with metabolic syndrome and raised serum transaminases, who drinks no alcohol, has fat in the liver (steatosis) on ultrasound: non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD). Why is this finding important?","o":["A. Fat in the liver proves that cirrhosis is already present","B. Fat in the liver is the mark of viral hepatitis in the periportal (zone 1) hepatocytes","C. Fatty liver with steatohepatitic injury (nonalcoholic steatohepatitis, NASH) carries an increased risk of advanced fibrosis, cirrhosis and carcinoma","D. Fat in the liver shows that a gallstone is blocking the common bile duct"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Fatty liver with steatohepatitic injury (nonalcoholic steatohepatitis, NASH) carries an increased risk of advanced fibrosis, cirrhosis and carcinoma","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Fatty liver with steatohepatitic injury raises the risk of advanced fibrosis, cirrhosis and carcinoma.</b> Fat alone can stay quiet. When it comes with <b>inflammation and injury of hepatocytes</b> (nonalcoholic steatohepatitis), activated stellate cells lay down collagen, and the scarring can progress.<br>A. Fat does not prove cirrhosis: cirrhosis is the end stage, and most fatty livers have little or no scar.<br>B. Viral hepatitis is a different disease that injures the periportal (zone 1) hepatocytes; fat builds up first around the central vein (zone 3).<br>D. A stone in the common bile duct causes jaundice and widened bile ducts, not a fatty liver.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)","H16 slide 61 (Clinical correlates)","H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2026","sl":3,"f":"G115"}],"tier":1,"n":"68"},{"id":"G115-3-2","s":"Ultrasound shows a fatty liver in a man with metabolic syndrome and raised serum transaminases who drinks no alcohol (non-alcoholic fatty liver disease, NAFLD). How can his physician determine whether he has nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH), with its risk of advanced liver disease?","o":["A. Repeat the abdominal ultrasound","B. Measure the serum transaminase levels again","C. Liver biopsy","D. Check the metabolic syndrome criteria (waist circumference, fasting glucose, triglycerides)"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Liver biopsy","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Liver biopsy.</b> Steatohepatitis is defined by what the tissue shows: <b>fat droplets in hepatocytes, clusters of inflammatory cells, and fibrosis around the hepatocytes</b> (a blue 'spider web' on a trichrome stain). Only a biopsy shows these, so it is the gold standard.<br>A. Ultrasound shows only that fat is present.<br>B. Transaminases show that hepatocytes are leaking enzymes, not whether there is inflammation or scar.<br>D. The metabolic syndrome criteria describe the patient's risk, not the state of the liver.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)","H16 slide 61 (Clinical correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2026","sl":3,"f":"G115"}],"tier":1,"n":"69"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":31,"label":"Bile canaliculus on a slide: thin channel between hepatocytes","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G116-42.jpg","cap":"Bile canaliculi between hepatocytes: faint on H&E, clear at high power and electron microscopy","where":"2026 lab, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"G118-2-1","s":"Identify the structure in the blue outline in the image below.","o":["A. Bile canaliculi","B. Sinusoidal space","C. Splenic sinus","D. Marrow cavity"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Bile canaliculi","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Bile canaliculi.</b> The blue outline traces a <b>thin, branching channel that runs between the facing sides of neighboring hepatocytes</b>, inside the plate. That is a bile canaliculus: a tiny groove with no lining cells of its own, sealed by tight junctions.<br>B. Sinusoidal space: the much wider pale channels between the plates, lined by endothelium and holding blood cells.<br>C. Splenic sinus: a blood channel of the red pulp of the spleen; this field is all hepatocytes.<br>D. Marrow cavity: the space inside a bone, filled with blood-forming cells and fat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G118-2.jpg","in":"Liver at high power (University of Wisconsin slide 20): hepatocytes with pale sinusoids between the plates; a blue line traces a thin, branching pink channel that runs between the faces of neighbouring hepatocytes (bile canaliculus); a dark blue box hides the label.","tg":["H16 slide 54 (BILIARY PASSAGE)","H16 slide 48 (HEPATOCYTES)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":2,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"70"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":31,"label":"Blood to central vein; bile out to triad","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G020-47.jpg","cap":"Blood flows portal field to central vein; bile flows back toward ductule","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 47","lg":[["arrows","flow: blood (blue venous, red arterial) along the sinusoid into the central vein; bile (green) the other way, to the bile ductule"]]},{"src":"../sl/G020-56.jpg","cap":"Classic lobule, portal lobule and acinus compared: shapes, centers, roles","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 56"},{"src":"../sl/G021-41.jpg","cap":"Blood and bile flow in opposite directions along the hepatocyte plate","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 41","lg":[["white arrows (blue sinusoids)","blood flow from portal vein and hepatic artery branches to the central vein"],["white arrows (green channel)","bile flow toward the bile duct"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G024-26-1","s":"Which of the following statements is correct regarding the structure of liver?","o":["A. Terminal hepatic venule (central vein) is located at the center of the portal lobule","B. The zone 1 is more susceptible to hypoxic injury","C. Hepatic arteriole carries blood rich in nutrients absorbed from GI tract","D. The flow of bile is always directed towards the center of classic hepatic lobule","E. Hepatic sinusoids drain into the central vein"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic sinusoids drain into the central vein","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Sinusoids drain into the central vein</b>: blood runs from the portal triad at the lobule edge, through the sinusoids, into the central vein.<br>A. The central vein is the center of the <b>classic</b> lobule; the portal lobule is centered on a portal triad.<br>B. <b>Zone 3</b> (by the central vein) is the hypoxia-prone zone; zone 1 gets the most oxygen.<br>C. The hepatic arteriole brings oxygen; the <b>portal venule</b> brings absorbed nutrients.<br>D. Bile flows <b>outward</b> to the portal triad, opposite to blood.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 56 (Classical lobule - Anatomical)","H16 slide 47 (FLOW OF BLOOD AND BILE)","H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2026","sl":22,"f":"G115"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":26,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":26,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":34,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":26,"f":"G025"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":28,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":1,"n":"71"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":32,"label":"Zone 3 least oxygen → hypoxic injury","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G020-59.jpg","cap":"Acinus zones: zone 1 by triad, zone 3 by central vein, hypoxia-prone","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 59"},{"src":"../sl/G024-15.jpg","cap":"Zone 3 circled at the central vein; zone 1 beside the portal tract","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 15","lg":[["1","zone 1 (nearest the portal triad; most oxygen)"],["2","zone 2 (intermediate)"],["3","zone 3 (around the central vein; least oxygen)"],["circles","zone 3 around each central vein"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0753-99-1","s":"Which zone has the poorest oxygen supply?","o":["A. 1","B. 2","C. 3"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"3 (zone 3, the zone next to the central vein)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Zone 3</b> (centrilobular), nearest the central vein. Oxygen-rich arterial blood enters at the portal triad and is used up along the sinusoid, so zone 3 gets the <b>poorest oxygen</b>; it does more anaerobic work and detoxification and is first hurt by hypoxia.<br>A. Zone 1 (periportal): next to the triad, richest oxygen; does protein synthesis.<br>B. Zone 2: intermediate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/F0753-99-1.jpg","in":"Two pictures. Left: drawing of two classic liver lobules with the acinus zones numbered 1, 2, 3 from the line between two portal triads out to each central vein. Right: liver H&E with a portal tract at lower left and a central vein at upper right; the numbers 1, 2, 3 sit along the line between them (1 next to the portal tract, 3 next to the central vein). The question asks which numbered zone gets the least oxygen. The slide's question and answer text are white on a white background, so the slide picture shows no answer. Same H&E micrograph as the bank card F0753-99-1 (checked against F0753 slide 99) and the same slide as G021 slide 40.","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty review","n":"Digestive system supplemental review Fall 2026","sl":42,"f":"G117"},{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":57,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":99,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2025 review","n":"Digestive system supplemental review Fall 2025","sl":40,"f":"G021"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":12,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Digestive system review 2023","sl":47,"f":"F0744"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":202,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["1","zone 1 (next to the portal tract, lower left; most oxygen)"],["2","zone 2 (intermediate)"],["3","zone 3 (next to the central vein, upper right; least oxygen)"]],"n":"72"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":32,"label":"Zone 3: acetaminophen, alcohol, fat, toxins","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G020-59.jpg","cap":"Zone 3 by central vein: detoxification, hypoxia, fat, acetaminophen and alcohol damage","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 59"},{"src":"../sl/G024-19.jpg","cap":"Zone III: least oxygen, drug metabolism; vulnerable to ischemic and toxic injury","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 19","lg":[["1","zone 1 (nearest the portal triad; most oxygen)"],["2","zone 2 (intermediate)"],["3","zone 3 (around the central vein; least oxygen)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G024-18-1","s":"A 65 year-old man undergoes an uncomplicated open heart operation for replacement of a stenotic aortic valve. Post-operatively, he is prescribed extra strength acetaminophen (Tylenol) for pain control. Routine measurement of serum transaminase measurements on post-operative day 5 reveal elevations to five times the normal values indicating hepatocellular injury. Which of the acinar zones shown below would be the most likely site of injury?","o":["A. Zone 1","B. Zone 2","C. Zone 3"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Zone 3","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Zone 3</b> (centrilobular, around the central vein). It gets the <b>least oxygen</b> and holds the most drug-metabolizing enzymes, so it is hit by both <b>ischemia</b> (heart surgery, low blood pressure) and <b>acetaminophen</b> (Tylenol) toxicity.<br>A. Zone 1 (periportal): most oxygen; injured first in viral hepatitis.<br>B. Zone 2 (intermediate): not the first site for either problem.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Liver, H&E: a portal tract at lower left and a central vein at upper right; the numbers 1, 2, 3 are placed along the line from the portal tract to the central vein. Same micrograph as G024 slide 18. Not needed to answer.","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2026","sl":14,"f":"G115"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":18,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":18,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":23,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":18,"f":"G025"}],"tier":1,"n":"73"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":33,"label":"Gallbladder: folded mucosa, no true villi","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G020-74.jpg","cap":"Gallbladder: branching mucosal folds that mimic villi, no glands beneath","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 74"},{"src":"../sl/G020-65.jpg","cap":"Mucosa folds mimic villi; simple columnar epithelium with microvilli","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 65","lg":[["LP","lamina propria"],["(a) arrows","simple columnar epithelium"],["M","muscularis (bracket line)"],["A","adventitia (bracket line)"],["G","mucus secretory granules"],["MV","microvilli"],["(b) arrows and arrowheads","spaces between folded lateral cell membranes (water leaves the cells here)"]]},{"src":"../sl/G024-31.jpg","cap":"Folded mucosa, lamina propria, random muscle bundles, adventitia against the liver","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 31","lg":[["arrows","simple columnar epithelium covering the mucosal folds"],["E","epithelium (simple columnar)"],["LP","lamina propria"],["SM","smooth muscle layer (muscularis; bundles run in all directions)"],["Ad","adventitia (where it lies against the liver)"],["BM","basement membrane"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G024-30-1","s":"A 45 year-old man with a history of cholelithiasis presents to the ED with severe right upper quadrant abdominal pain radiating to the right shoulder, nausea and fever. He is taken to the operating room where a cholecystectomy is performed. The resected gallbladder and a representative histologic section are displayed at right. All of the following are true EXCEPT:","o":["A. The gallbladder stores and concentrates bile","B. Inflammation of the gallbladder is usually associated with gallstones","C. The gallbladder mucosa contains villi which increase mucosal surface area","D. The gallbladder is covered partly by serosa and partly adventitia"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The gallbladder mucosa contains villi which increase mucosal surface area","a":"C","why":"<b>C is false.</b> The gallbladder has <b>no true villi</b>; its mucosa is thrown into <b>folds that mimic villi</b> when the organ is empty. It concentrates bile by absorbing water, not by absorbing food.<br>A. True: it stores and concentrates bile.<br>B. True: cholecystitis is usually caused by gallstones blocking the cystic duct.<br>D. True: serosa on the free (peritoneal) surface, adventitia where it attaches to the liver.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Opened gallbladder packed with faceted brown gallstones (top) and a section of the inflamed gallbladder wall (bottom; Sternberg's Diagnostic Surgical Pathology, Fig. 38-9). Not needed to answer.","tg":["H16 slide 64 (Gall bladder)","H16 slide 65 (Mucosa)","H16 slide 66 (Function & Clinical correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2026","sl":28,"f":"G115"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":30,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":30,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":45,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":30,"f":"G025"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":32,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":1,"n":"74"}]}],"sum":{},"units":[{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The lingual papillae","p":"<p>Papillae are raised folds of the epithelium, each with a core of connective tissue, on the dorsal surface of the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. There are <b>four types</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G018-17.jpg\" alt=\"The four papillae: filiform, foliate, fungiform, circumvallate\"><figcaption>The four papillae: filiform, foliate, fungiform, circumvallate <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>(c) arrows</b> deep clefts between the foliate ridges<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>(b) arrows</b> secondary connective tissue papillae pushing up into the epithelium</div></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>Shape and location</th><th>Keratin</th><th>Taste buds</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Filiform</b></td><td>The most numerous. Slender, pointed cones in rows over the whole dorsal surface. They give the tongue its rough texture and grip food (friction) during chewing.</td><td>Heavily keratinized tips</td><td><b>None</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Fungiform</b></td><td>Mushroom-shaped: narrow stalk, broad round top with a connective tissue core. Scattered singly among the filiform papillae, more near the tip.</td><td>Thin</td><td>A few, on the upper surface</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Circumvallate</b> (vallate)</td><td>The largest: 8–12 domes in a V just in front of the sulcus terminalis. Each sits level with the surface, ringed by a deep trench (a moat).</td><td>None</td><td>Many, in the epithelium of the side walls facing the trench</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Foliate</b></td><td>Rows of parallel, leaf-like ridges on the sides of the back of the tongue, separated by deep clefts. Well developed in children, small in adults.</td><td>None</td><td>Many, in the walls of the clefts</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Von Ebner glands</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Small <b>serous</b> (watery) salivary glands in the tongue under the circumvallate papillae.</li>\n<li>Their ducts empty into the base of the trench. The steady flow rinses the trench, clearing old food so the taste buds can sense new tastes.</li>\n<li>They also secrete lingual lipase.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>On a slide</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Rows of pointed, keratinized spikes: filiform.</li>\n<li>One broad mushroom among spikes: fungiform.</li>\n<li>One large dome in a trench, with glands below: circumvallate.</li>\n<li>A row of parallel ridges with clefts: foliate.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The oral cavity","sn":2,"pn":"2.4"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Taste buds","p":"<p>A <b>taste bud</b> is a pale, oval cluster of spindle-shaped cells set inside the stratified epithelium. It reaches from the basal lamina to the surface, where it opens through a small <b>taste pore</b> (gustatory pore).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G015-36.jpg\" alt=\"Taste bud: gustatory pore, taste cells with microvilli, supporting and basal cells\"><figcaption>Taste bud: gustatory pore, taste cells with microvilli, supporting and basal cells <small>· 2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 36</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Where taste buds are</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Side walls of the circumvallate papillae: the most, about 250 per papilla.</li>\n<li>Walls of the foliate clefts, and the tops of fungiform papillae (a few).</li>\n<li>None on the filiform papillae.</li>\n<li>Also scattered in the soft palate, pharynx and epiglottis.</li>\n<li>None on the lips, although the lips have the same kind of epithelium. None in the stomach, intestine or appendix, which are lined by a single layer of columnar cells.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On a slide: pale ovals lined up inside the stratified squamous epithelium of a wall that faces a deep cleft are taste buds, so the tissue is tongue.</p>\n<h4>Cells of a taste bud</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Gustatory (taste, sensory) cells</b>: long, pale cells. Their apical <b>microvilli</b> (not cilia, not stereocilia) reach into the taste pore. There, dissolved substances bind receptors on the microvilli. At their base the cells synapse with afferent nerve fibers.</li>\n<li><b>Supporting (sustentacular) cells</b>: long cells between the taste cells.</li>\n<li><b>Basal cells</b>: small stem cells at the base of the bud. They replace the taste cells, which live only about 10 days.</li>\n<li>Afferent (sensory) nerve fibers enter the bud at its base.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Tastes</h4>\n<p>Salty, sweet, bitter, sour and umami (the taste of glutamate). Water, calcium and fat may be extra tastes (lecture slide 30).</p>","s":"The oral cavity","sn":2,"pn":"2.5"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Lining, glands and outer coat","p":"<p>The esophagus is a muscular tube about 25 cm (10 inches) long. It carries food from the pharynx to the stomach. An upper and a lower esophageal sphincter close its two ends. Its wall follows the four-layer plan.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G018-23.jpg\" alt=\"Esophagus: epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae, and mucous glands in the submucosa\"><figcaption>Esophagus: epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae, and mucous glands in the submucosa <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 23</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>SS</b> stratified squamous epithelium, nonkeratinized<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>LP</b> lamina propria<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>MM</b> muscularis mucosae (smooth muscle)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>D</b> ducts of the esophageal glands<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>GL</b> esophageal mucous glands in the submucosa</div></figure>\n<h4>Mucosa</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Epithelium: thick <b>nonkeratinized stratified squamous</b>. The surface cells keep their nuclei. It resists the rubbing of food. Like all stratified squamous linings, it has no goblet cells.</li>\n<li>Lamina propria: loose connective tissue. Near the stomach it holds small mucous glands, the esophageal cardiac glands.</li>\n<li>Muscularis mucosae: a single layer of longitudinal smooth muscle (incomplete near the top).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Submucosa</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Esophageal glands</b>: mucous glands that lubricate the lining. Their ducts cross the mucosa to the lumen.</li>\n<li>A rich plexus of veins. When pressure in the portal vein rises (portal hypertension, for example from alcoholic cirrhosis), these submucosal veins swell into <b>esophageal varices</b>. They can burst and cause vomiting of blood.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Muscularis externa</h4>\n<p>Inner circular and outer longitudinal layers. The type of muscle changes along the length (3.2).</p>\n<h4>Outer coat</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Adventitia along most of its length, in the neck and chest.</li>\n<li>Serosa only on the short segment below the diaphragm (the last 1–2 cm), which lies in the peritoneal cavity.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Recognizing esophagus on a slide</h4>\n<p>Thick nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, mucous glands in the submucosa, and a thick muscularis externa.</p>\n<h4>Infection of the lining</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>In people with weak immunity (for example AIDS, acquired immunodeficiency syndrome), the yeast Candida can grow on the lining. It forms raised <b>white plaques</b> on red, swollen mucosa and makes swallowing painful.</li>\n<li>Herpes simplex virus instead makes small punched-out ulcers.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The esophagus","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The muscle changes from top to bottom","p":"<p>The esophagus is divided into thirds by the type of muscle in its <b>muscularis externa</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G015-60.jpg\" alt=\"Esophagus: skeletal, mixed and smooth muscle by thirds\"><figcaption>Esophagus: skeletal, mixed and smooth muscle by thirds <small>· 2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 60</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Third</th><th>Muscularis externa</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Upper</td><td>Skeletal muscle (swallowing starts as a voluntary act)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Middle</td><td>Skeletal and smooth muscle mixed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lower</td><td>Smooth muscle only, like the rest of the gut</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The esophagus is the <b>only part of the digestive tube with skeletal muscle in its muscularis externa</b>. (The external anal sphincter is skeletal too, but it is a separate sphincter muscle.)</li>\n<li>Skeletal fibers: large, pink, cross-striated, with many nuclei at the edge of each fiber.</li>\n<li>Smooth muscle cells: small spindles with one central nucleus.</li>\n<li>Cut lengthwise, skeletal fibers are wide bands crossed by fine stripes (striations); smooth muscle has no stripes. Both kinds side by side in one field means the middle third.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Reading a cross-section of the tube</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The inner circular layer is cut lengthwise, so its cells look long.</li>\n<li>The outer longitudinal layer is cut across, so its fibers look like round or polygonal profiles.</li>\n<li>The myenteric plexus sits in the thin connective tissue between them.</li>\n<li>Do not confuse the muscularis externa with the muscularis mucosae, the thin band of smooth muscle right under the epithelium.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Lower esophageal sphincter and achalasia</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>At the lower end, the circular muscle stays contracted as the <b>lower esophageal sphincter</b>. During swallowing, nerve cells of the myenteric plexus make it relax.</li>\n<li>In <b>achalasia</b>, the myenteric plexus nerve cells at the sphincter are lost. The sphincter cannot relax. Food piles up, and the esophagus above it widens (a bird's-beak shape on a contrast X-ray). Swallowing of both solids and liquids gets harder.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The esophagus","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Reflux, esophagitis and metaplasia","p":"<h4>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Acid from the stomach flows back into the lower esophagus when the lower esophageal sphincter does not close well.</li>\n<li>Triggers: large meals, lying down after eating, obesity, coffee, alcohol, smoking.</li>\n<li>Symptoms: heartburn (burning pain behind the breastbone) and a sour taste, eased by antacids.</li>\n<li>Treatment starts with lifestyle changes (smaller meals, no eating before lying down, weight loss, less coffee and alcohol), then acid-lowering drugs (proton pump inhibitors).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Reflux esophagitis on a biopsy</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Basal cell hyperplasia: the basal layer of the squamous epithelium thickens.</li>\n<li>The lamina propria papillae stretch up toward the surface.</li>\n<li>Inflammatory cells (eosinophils, neutrophils, lymphocytes) appear inside the epithelium.</li>\n<li>Other causes of esophagitis to rule out: eosinophilic (allergic) esophagitis, infections (Candida, herpes, cytomegalovirus), pills that stick in the esophagus, and radiation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Metaplasia</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Metaplasia</b> is the replacement of one mature cell type by another mature type that better withstands a chronic injury. It can reverse if the injury stops.</li>\n<li>Example in the airway: in smokers, ciliated pseudostratified epithelium becomes stratified squamous.</li>\n<li>Compare: dysplasia is disordered growth of abnormal cells (a precancer); neoplasia is new, uncontrolled growth (a tumor).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Barrett esophagus</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Years of acid reflux turn the stratified squamous lining of the <b>distal esophagus</b>, just above the esophagogastric junction, into <b>simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells</b> (intestinal-type metaplasia). Columnar cells resist acid better.</li>\n<li>The normal esophagus is squamous all the way down to the junction.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G015-66.jpg\" alt=\"Normal esophagus (left) and Barrett esophagus (right)\"><figcaption>Normal esophagus (left) and Barrett esophagus (right) <small>· 2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 66</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"The esophagus","sn":3,"pn":"3.4"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Barrett esophagus in the clinic, and cancer","p":"<h4>Recognizing Barrett esophagus</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Story: long-standing heartburn, worse after meals or lying down.</li>\n<li>Endoscopy: <b>salmon-pink or red, velvety mucosa</b> reaching up from the junction into the lower esophagus. Normal squamous lining is pale pinkish-white.</li>\n<li>Biopsy: columnar cells <b>with goblet cells</b> where squamous epithelium should be.</li>\n<li>Cause: the esophagus is exposed to refluxed gastric acid (and bile) for years. It is not caused by Helicobacter pylori, and it is not a motility problem.</li>\n<li>Goblet cells are the key. Stomach mucosa (including the cardia) has no goblet cells. So columnar mucosa with goblet cells in the esophagus is intestinal metaplasia, not misplaced stomach lining.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Where Barrett can lead</h4>\n<p>Barrett esophagus → low-grade dysplasia → high-grade dysplasia → invasive <b>adenocarcinoma</b>, a cancer of gland-forming columnar cells.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G015-65.jpg\" alt=\"Normal lining → Barrett → dysplasia → invasive carcinoma\"><figcaption>Normal lining → Barrett → dysplasia → invasive carcinoma <small>· 2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 65</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Adenocarcinoma</th><th>Squamous cell carcinoma</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Starts from</td><td>Barrett (columnar) epithelium</td><td>The normal squamous epithelium</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Where</td><td>Lower third</td><td>Upper and middle thirds</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Main risk factors</td><td>Chronic reflux and Barrett esophagus, obesity, smoking</td><td>Smoking, alcohol</td></tr>\n</table></div>","s":"The esophagus","sn":3,"pn":"3.5"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The overall design of the stomach","p":"<ul>\n<li>The widest part of the tube; it holds about 1.5 L.</li>\n<li>It mixes food with acid and enzymes into a soupy paste called chyme.</li>\n<li>Four regions: cardia, fundus, body and pylorus.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G018-29.jpg\" alt=\"Stomach wall: rugae, gastric pits and glands, muscularis mucosae, submucosa, muscularis externa, serosa\"><figcaption>Stomach wall: rugae, gastric pits and glands, muscularis mucosae, submucosa, muscularis externa, serosa <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 29</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Special features</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>What it is</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Rugae</b></td><td>Large folds of mucosa with a core of submucosa. They flatten as the stomach fills. Only the stomach has them. (The small intestine has permanent circular folds instead, 5.1.)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Gastric pits</b></td><td>Funnel-shaped dips of the surface epithelium into the mucosa. The gastric glands open into the bottom of the pits.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Gastric glands</b></td><td>Tubular glands in the lamina propria that secrete the gastric juice.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>The wall, layer by layer</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Mucosa: simple columnar epithelium of <b>surface mucous cells</b>. Each cell has a pale cap of mucus at its top. There are no goblet cells in the stomach. The lamina propria is squeezed into thin strips between the packed glands. Then a muscularis mucosae.</li>\n<li>Submucosa: connective tissue; it forms the core of the rugae.</li>\n<li>Muscularis externa: <b>three layers</b>: inner oblique, middle circular and outer longitudinal. The extra layer helps churn food.</li>\n<li>Serosa (not adventitia): the stomach hangs in the peritoneal cavity.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The stomach","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Three regions, three kinds of mucosa","p":"<p>Under the microscope, the stomach has three kinds of mucosa. Compare <b>pit depth with gland length</b>. Going from the esophagus toward the duodenum, the pits get deeper and the glands get shorter, but more branched and coiled.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G015-75.jpg\" alt=\"Cardia, body/fundus and pylorus\"><figcaption>Cardia, body/fundus and pylorus <small>· 2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 75</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>arrowhead (cardia panel)</b> where the pit&#x27;s mucous lining meets the gland (neck)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>P</b> deep gastric pits<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>G</b> short coiled pyloric glands (not G cells)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>LP</b> lamina propria</div></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Region</th><th>Pits</th><th>Glands</th><th>Main cells</th><th>Job</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Cardia</b>: a narrow ring, 1.5–3 cm wide, around the entry of the esophagus</td><td>Fairly long</td><td>Short, coiled mucous glands</td><td>Mucous cells; few or no parietal and chief cells; no G cells</td><td>Protection (mucus)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Fundus and body</b>: most of the stomach (oxyntic, meaning acid-making, mucosa)</td><td>Short (about a quarter of the mucosa)</td><td>Long, straight, tightly packed</td><td>Parietal cells (pink) and chief cells (purple)</td><td>Digestion: acid and pepsinogen</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Pylorus</b> (antrum and pyloric canal)</td><td>Deep (about half the mucosa)</td><td>Short, branched and coiled mucous glands</td><td>Mucous cells (mucus and lysozyme); <b>G cells</b>; no parietal or chief cells</td><td>Mucus to protect the duodenum; gastrin</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The lab deck sums it up: the cardia mucosa looks like the pyloric mucosa but has no G cells.</li>\n<li>G cells release the hormone <b>gastrin</b> into the blood. Gastrin tells the body and fundus to make acid and makes the stomach move more.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Every region is still stomach on a slide</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>All three regions share one surface: a sheet of tall, pale surface mucous cells that dips into pits, with no goblet cells and no villi.</li>\n<li>So deep pits over pale, coiled mucous glands, with no pink parietal cells in sight, is still stomach (pylorus or cardia).</li>\n<li>Look-alikes: the colon has a flat surface over straight crypts packed with goblet cells; the jejunum has villi; the esophagus has stratified squamous epithelium.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The stomach","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Gland cells of the body and fundus","p":"<p>A gland of the body or fundus has three regions: the pit at the top, a narrow neck (isthmus), and the long body and base. Each region has its own cells.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G018-30.jpg\" alt=\"Fundic mucosa: pits, neck and glands; surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal and chief cells\"><figcaption>Fundic mucosa: pits, neck and glands; surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal and chief cells <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 30</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>GP</b> gastric pits<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>SM</b> surface mucous cells<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>MN</b> mucous neck cells<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>P</b> parietal cells (big, pink, round &#x27;fried egg&#x27;)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>C</b> chief cells (purple, basophilic, deep in the glands)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>MM</b> muscularis mucosae<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>boxes</b> areas enlarged at right</div></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Cell</th><th>Where</th><th>Look on H&amp;E</th><th>Secretes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Surface mucous cell</td><td>Surface and pits</td><td>Tall columnar, pale mucus cap on top</td><td>Thick, sticky, <b>alkaline</b> mucus that clings to the surface and protects it from acid</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mucous neck cell</td><td>Neck</td><td>Smaller, pale</td><td>Watery, less alkaline (more acidic) mucus</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Stem cell</td><td>Neck (isthmus)</td><td>Small, dividing</td><td>Nothing; it replaces the other cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Parietal (oxyntic) cell</b></td><td>Mostly the upper half of the gland</td><td>Large, round, <b>pink (acidophilic)</b>, central nucleus; packed with mitochondria</td><td><b>Hydrochloric acid (HCl)</b> and <b>intrinsic factor</b>, which the ileum needs to absorb vitamin B12</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Chief (zymogenic) cell</b></td><td>Base of the gland</td><td><b>Purple-blue (basophilic)</b> from rough endoplasmic reticulum; zymogen granules at the top</td><td><b>Pepsinogen</b> (becomes pepsin in acid; digests protein) and a lipase precursor (gastric lipase; starts fat digestion)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Enteroendocrine cell</td><td>Scattered in the glands</td><td>Pale; needs special stains (4.4)</td><td>Hormones and local signals</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>On a slide, go by size and colour, not only by position: the big, round, pink cells with a central nucleus are parietal cells, and the smaller blue-purple cells squeezed between them are chief cells. Deep in a gland the two are often mixed.</p>\n<p>Starch digestion is not a stomach job: amylase comes from the salivary glands and the pancreas. Secretin and cholecystokinin (CCK) are not made by the gastric glands either: they are hormones from enteroendocrine cells of the small intestine (5.3).</p>","s":"The stomach","sn":4,"pn":"4.3"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Enteroendocrine cells, and the parietal cell at work","p":"<h4>Enteroendocrine cells</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Small cells scattered among the gland cells, from the stomach to the colon. Their hormone granules sit at the <b>base</b> of the cell. They release into the blood (endocrine) or onto nearby cells (paracrine), not into the lumen.</li>\n<li>On hematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E) they look pale and are easy to miss. They are shown with <b>special stains</b>: chromium salts (enterochromaffin cells), silver salts (argentaffin cells), or <b>immunostaining</b> (immunohistochemistry) for their hormones.</li>\n<li>Open type: reaches the lumen with microvilli that sense nutrients, much like taste cells. Closed type: does not reach the lumen.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Cell</th><th>Product</th><th>Effect</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Enterochromaffin (EC) cell</td><td>Serotonin</td><td>Increases gut motility</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Enterochromaffin-like cell</td><td>Histamine (paracrine)</td><td>Increases acid secretion by parietal cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td>G cell (pylorus)</td><td>Gastrin</td><td>Increases acid secretion and motility</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Small-intestine cells</td><td>Cholecystokinin, secretin and others</td><td>See 5.3</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>The parietal cell at rest and at work</h4>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G018-33.jpg\" alt=\"Parietal cell, resting and active\"><figcaption>Parietal cell, resting and active <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 33</small></figcaption></figure>\n<ul>\n<li>Resting: many tubulovesicles near the top, full of proton pumps; short intracellular canaliculi; few microvilli.</li>\n<li>Active: the tubulovesicles fuse with the cell membrane. They form deep <b>intracellular canaliculi</b> lined with many microvilli. This greatly enlarges the membrane that carries the H+/K+ pumps (proton pumps). Many mitochondria supply the energy.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The stomach","sn":4,"pn":"4.4"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Diseases of the stomach","p":"<h4>Pernicious anemia: the chain of events</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Parietal cells are destroyed or bypassed.</li>\n<li>No <b>intrinsic factor</b> is made. Intrinsic factor normally binds vitamin B12 so the ileum can absorb it.</li>\n<li>Vitamin B12 deficiency follows.</li>\n<li>Red cell precursors cannot make DNA normally, so they grow large: <b>megaloblastic (macrocytic) anemia</b>. The blood smear shows large oval red cells and hypersegmented neutrophils (6 or more nuclear lobes).</li>\n<li>Other effects: fatigue, a smooth red tongue, diarrhea, and nerve damage (numbness, tingling, poor balance).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Size clue: lack of B12 or folate gives big red cells; lack of iron gives small red cells.</p>\n<h4>Two causes</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Autoimmune atrophic gastritis</b> (true pernicious anemia): antibodies attack the parietal cells (their H+/K+ proton pump) and intrinsic factor. The mucosa of the body and fundus thins. It often comes with other autoimmune diseases, such as type 1 diabetes and Hashimoto thyroiditis.</li>\n<li><b>Stomach bypass surgery</b>: food skips most of the stomach, so the parietal cells no longer supply intrinsic factor. The lecture lists bypass as a cause of pernicious anemia.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Helicobacter pylori</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Curved, rod-shaped bacteria that live in the surface mucus.</li>\n<li>The usual cause of chronic gastritis and <b>peptic ulcer disease</b>.</li>\n<li>Long-term infection builds lymphoid tissue in the mucosa and raises the risk of <b>gastric lymphoma</b> (MALT lymphoma) and of gastric adenocarcinoma.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>A smooth muscle tumor</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <b>leiomyoma</b> is a benign tumor of smooth muscle, usually from the muscularis externa. It forms a small, well-defined mass under the mucosa, made of bland spindle cells in bundles that stain for smooth muscle markers (desmin, smooth muscle actin).</li>\n<li>Its look-alike, the gastrointestinal stromal tumor (GIST), comes from the pacemaker cells of the gut wall (interstitial cells of Cajal) and stains for KIT (CD117).</li>\n</ul>","s":"The stomach","sn":4,"pn":"4.5"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Three levels of folding, and the crypts","p":"<p>The small intestine (duodenum, jejunum, ileum) finishes digestion, absorbs the products, and releases hormones. Its design is all about <b>surface area</b>. Three levels of folding multiply the absorbing surface.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G018-37.jpg\" alt=\"Plicae circulares, villi and microvilli\"><figcaption>Plicae circulares, villi and microvilli <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 37</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Level</th><th>Structure</th><th>Core</th><th>How it looks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Gross</td><td><b>Plicae circulares</b> (valves of Kerckring): permanent circular folds</td><td><b>Submucosa</b></td><td>Tall folds of the whole mucosa, each covered with many villi</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Light microscope</td><td><b>Villi</b>: finger-like or leaf-like projections of the mucosa</td><td><b>Lamina propria</b>, with capillaries, a central lacteal and strands of smooth muscle</td><td>Small projections, each covered by one layer of columnar cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Electron microscope</td><td><b>Microvilli</b> on the top of each absorptive cell</td><td>Actin filaments</td><td>Together they form the brush (striated) border, a thin fuzzy pink line on the cell tops</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Plicae circulares start in the duodenum, are tallest and most numerous in the jejunum, and fade out in the distal ileum. They do not flatten, unlike the rugae of the stomach.</li>\n<li>The number of villi and microvilli falls along the way: duodenum &gt; jejunum &gt; ileum.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Crypts of Lieberkühn (intestinal glands)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Simple tubular glands. The epithelium dips down between the villi as far as the muscularis mucosae. So they lie in the <b>mucosa</b>, not the submucosa.</li>\n<li>Present along the whole small intestine and the large intestine. (The colon keeps its crypts but has no villi.) The esophagus has none, and the stomach has gastric glands instead.</li>\n<li>They hold the stem cells, Paneth cells and many enteroendocrine cells (5.3).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>On a slide</h4>\n<p>Villi project above the mucosa. The crypts are the straight tubes below the villi, just above the muscularis mucosae. Glands below the muscularis mucosae, in the submucosa, are something else: the Brunner glands of the duodenum (5.4).</p>","s":"The small intestine","sn":5,"pn":"5.1"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Enterocytes and fat absorption","p":"<h4>Cells of the intestinal lining</h4>\n<p>Five cell types line the villi and crypts: enterocytes (by far the most numerous), goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells and stem cells. This part covers the first two; 5.3 covers the rest.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G018-38.jpg\" alt=\"Small intestine cell types\"><figcaption>Small intestine cell types <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 38</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Enterocytes (columnar absorptive cells)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Tall columnar cells with a nucleus near the base and a dense <b>brush border of microvilli</b> on top.</li>\n<li>Enzymes in the brush border (for sugars and short peptides) do the last step of digestion right at the cell surface (<b>terminal digestion</b>). Then the cell absorbs the products.</li>\n<li>Tight junctions seal the cells together, so absorbed material passes through the cells.</li>\n<li>Life cycle: stem cells in the crypts divide; the new cells climb the villus and are shed at its tip after about 5 days. Cells do not divide on the villus itself.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>How enterocytes absorb fat</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>In the lumen, pancreatic lipase splits triglycerides into fatty acids and monoglycerides. Bile salts carry them in tiny droplets (micelles).</li>\n<li>These diffuse into the enterocyte (no active transport is needed).</li>\n<li>In the smooth endoplasmic reticulum, they are rebuilt into <b>triglycerides</b>.</li>\n<li>Proteins are added (rough endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi), packaging them as <b>chylomicrons</b>.</li>\n<li>Chylomicrons leave the base of the cell and enter the <b>lacteal</b>, the lymph capillary in the villus core, not the blood capillaries. They reach the blood later through the thoracic duct.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G018-40.jpg\" alt=\"Lipid absorption and processing by enterocytes\"><figcaption>Lipid absorption and processing by enterocytes <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 40</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>SER</b> smooth endoplasmic reticulum (packed with small lipid droplets in a)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>(a) arrows</b> lipid globules leaving the cell into the space between cells<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>RER</b> rough endoplasmic reticulum<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>1</b> bile acids and lipase split fats into glycerol, fatty acids, monoglycerides<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>2</b> triglycerides remade in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>3</b> triglyceride + protein coat = chylomicron<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>4</b> chylomicrons enter the lacteal (lymph)</div></figure>\n<h4>Goblet cells</h4>\n<p>Scattered among the enterocytes. They secrete mucins, which form the protective mucus layer. Their number rises along the gut: duodenum &lt; jejunum &lt; ileum &lt; colon.</p>\n<h4>Celiac disease</h4>\n<p>An immune reaction to gluten flattens the villi (villous atrophy), deepens the crypts, and fills the epithelium with lymphocytes. With fewer enterocytes and less brush border, nutrients are poorly absorbed: chronic diarrhea and weight loss.</p>","s":"The small intestine","sn":5,"pn":"5.2"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Crypt cells: stem cells, Paneth cells and hormone cells","p":"<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G016-17.jpg\" alt=\"Paneth cells at the base of a crypt\"><figcaption>Paneth cells at the base of a crypt <small>· 2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 17</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>A (arrowheads)</b> Paneth cells: big red granules (lysozyme, defensins) at the crypt base<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>circle</b> dividing cell (mitotic figure: clumped chromosomes with a clear halo); crypts are where new epithelial cells are made</div></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Cell</th><th>Where</th><th>Look</th><th>Job</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Stem (regenerative) cells</td><td>Lower part of the crypt</td><td>Small; dividing cells (mitotic figures) are often seen</td><td>Renew the whole lining every few days: mostly enterocytes, plus goblet, Paneth and enteroendocrine cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Paneth cells</b></td><td><b>Base of the crypts</b></td><td>Pyramidal cells packed with large, <b>bright red (eosinophilic) granules</b> at the top</td><td><b>Innate immunity</b>: release lysozyme, defensins and phospholipase A2, which kill bacteria. This keeps the crypt and its stem cells free of microbes and controls the number of gut bacteria.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Enteroendocrine cells</td><td>Crypts and villi</td><td>Pale; granules at the base; special stains</td><td>Hormones (below)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Goblet cells</td><td>Crypts and villi</td><td>Pale mucus-filled cup; nucleus at the base</td><td>Mucus</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Do not confuse the red apical granules of Paneth cells with the stomach's chief cells (purple, in gastric glands) or with enteroendocrine cells (pale, granules at the base).</p>\n<h4>Hormones of the small intestine</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Cholecystokinin (CCK)</b>: released when fat and protein reach the duodenum. It makes the gallbladder contract (bile release) and the pancreatic acinar cells secrete enzymes. It also slows the stomach and signals fullness.</li>\n<li><b>Secretin</b>: released when acid reaches the duodenum. It makes the pancreatic ducts secrete bicarbonate, which neutralizes the acid.</li>\n<li>Glucagon-like peptide 1 (GLP-1) and peptide YY: signal fullness (satiety) to the brain.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The small intestine","sn":5,"pn":"5.3"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Duodenum and jejunum","p":"<p>All three parts of the small intestine have villi and crypts. Each has one <b>signature feature</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G018-36.jpg\" alt=\"Duodenum (Brunner glands), jejunum (plicae), ileum (Peyer patches)\"><figcaption>Duodenum (Brunner glands), jejunum (plicae), ileum (Peyer patches) <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 36</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>dashed line</b> border between villi (above) and crypts (below)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>arrow</b> Brunner gland duct emptying into the base of a crypt<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>P</b> plica circularis (fold with a submucosa core)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>SM</b> submucosa<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>M</b> muscularis externa<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>large box</b> one Peyer patch lymphoid nodule with the mucosa over it<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>small box</b> edge of a lymphoid nodule against the submucosa</div></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Segment</th><th>Signature</th><th>Other clues</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Duodenum</b></td><td><b>Brunner glands</b> in the submucosa</td><td>Broad, leaf-like villi. Outer coat: serosa on the first part, adventitia on parts 2–4.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Jejunum</b></td><td>The <b>tallest, most numerous plicae circulares</b>, with a thin, plain submucosa and no glands</td><td>Long, finger-like villi; serosa</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Ileum</b></td><td><b>Peyer patches</b> in the lamina propria and submucosa (5.5)</td><td>Shorter villi; the most goblet cells of the small intestine; serosa</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Brunner glands</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Branched tubular <b>mucous glands in the submucosa</b> of the duodenum. Their ducts cross the muscularis mucosae and open into the crypts.</li>\n<li>They secrete <b>alkaline mucus</b> (pH about 9). It neutralizes the acid chyme arriving from the stomach and protects the duodenal lining.</li>\n<li>Exclusive to the duodenum. The only other glands in the submucosa of the tube are the esophageal glands.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>On a slide</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Villi plus pale mucous glands filling the submucosa: duodenum.</li>\n<li>Villi on tall folds with thin cores, and nothing in the submucosa: jejunum.</li>\n<li>Villi plus large lymphoid nodules: ileum.</li>\n<li>The pylorus also has mucous glands, but they sit in the mucosa under deep pits, and there are no villi.</li>\n<li>A fold that carries villi is a plica circularis, so the organ is small intestine. The folds of the stomach (rugae) carry pits, not villi. The colon and the appendix have no villi and no plicae.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The small intestine","sn":5,"pn":"5.4"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The colon's lining","p":"<p>The large intestine runs from the cecum through the ascending, transverse, descending (left) and sigmoid colon to the rectum. Its jobs are to <b>absorb water and electrolytes</b> and to form stool. Nutrients have already been absorbed in the small intestine.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Its absorptive cells pump sodium (Na+) out of the lumen actively. Water follows passively by osmosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G018-44.jpg\" alt=\"Large intestine mucosa: straight crypts, many goblet cells, no villi\"><figcaption>Large intestine mucosa: straight crypts, many goblet cells, no villi <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 44</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>arrows</b> gland openings on the surface<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>boxes</b> top: surface and gland tops, many goblet cells; bottom: gland bases at the muscularis mucosae<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>Gl</b> intestinal glands (crypts)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>LP</b> lamina propria<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>bracket (right edge)</b> mucosa (label cut off)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>SubM</b> submucosa<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>MM</b> muscularis mucosae<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>(d) L</b> folded cell-membrane leaflets in widened spaces between cells (water transport)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>(c) G</b> goblet cells</div></figure>\n<h4>Mucosa</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Simple columnar epithelium. The surface is smooth and flat, with <b>no villi</b> and no plicae.</li>\n<li>Straight, closely packed tubular <b>crypts of Lieberkühn</b> reach down to the muscularis mucosae. Cut across, they look like rows of round rings.</li>\n<li>Cells of the crypts:\n<ul>\n<li>Colonocytes (absorptive cells): short microvilli; wide spaces between neighboring cells, a sign of water transport.</li>\n<li><b>Goblet cells</b>: very many, more than anywhere else in the tube. Their mucus lubricates the drying stool and protects the lining.</li>\n<li>A few enteroendocrine cells.</li>\n<li>Stem cells at the base of the crypts.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>Lamina propria: rich in MALT (lymphocytes and lymphoid nodules).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>On a slide</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Goblet cells: pale, round, mucus-filled cups with the nucleus squeezed to the base.</li>\n<li>Stem cells: small, dark cells at the bottom of the crypts, sometimes dividing.</li>\n<li>A flat surface, straight crypts full of goblet cells, and no villi: colon.</li>\n<li>Features that belong to other organs, not the colon: rugae (stomach), villi (small intestine), Peyer patches (ileum).</li>\n</ul>","s":"The large intestine, appendix and anal canal","sn":6,"pn":"6.1"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The colon's wall: teniae coli and diverticula","p":"<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G016-26.jpg\" alt=\"Layers of the large intestine, with a tenia coli in the outer muscle layer\"><figcaption>Layers of the large intestine, with a tenia coli in the outer muscle layer <small>· 2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 26</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Teniae coli</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>In the colon, the <b>outer longitudinal layer of the muscularis externa</b> is gathered into <b>three narrow ribbons</b>, the teniae coli. Between them the longitudinal layer is very thin.</li>\n<li>The inner circular layer stays complete.</li>\n<li>The teniae are shorter than the colon, so they bunch it into pouches (haustra).</li>\n<li>The appendix and the rectum have no teniae; there the longitudinal layer is complete again.</li>\n<li>On a cross-section of colon: three thick bundles of longitudinal muscle, cut across, on the outside of the circular layer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Outer coat</h4>\n<p>Serosa where the colon hangs free (transverse and sigmoid colon). Adventitia where it is fixed to the back wall (ascending and descending colon).</p>\n<h4>Diverticula</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Small pouches of mucosa and submucosa pushed out through weak spots in the muscularis externa, where blood vessels pass through the wall, between the teniae.</li>\n<li>Most common in the sigmoid colon of older adults (high pressure inside, low-fiber diet).</li>\n<li>Diverticulosis can bleed: sudden, painless, bright red blood from the rectum. Inflamed diverticula (diverticulitis) cause left lower abdominal pain and fever.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The large intestine, appendix and anal canal","sn":6,"pn":"6.2"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Ducts inside the lobule: intercalated and striated","p":"<p>Saliva leaves the acinus through a chain of ducts. The first two lie inside the lobule (intralobular ducts), and they change the saliva as it passes.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Duct</th><th>Lining</th><th>Look</th><th>What it does to saliva</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Intercalated duct</b></td><td>Simple cuboidal (low)</td><td>Short and narrow, easy to miss; myoepithelial cells around it</td><td>Links each acinus to a striated duct. Absorbs chloride (Cl−) and secretes bicarbonate (HCO3−).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Striated duct</b></td><td>Simple columnar</td><td>Wide pink cells with round central nuclei and fine vertical stripes in the base (<b>basal striations</b>)</td><td>Actively reabsorbs sodium (Na+) and chloride, and secretes potassium (K+) and bicarbonate.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G020-13.jpg\" alt=\"Striated duct: basal infoldings lined with mitochondria\"><figcaption>Striated duct: basal infoldings lined with mitochondria <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 13</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>a</b> light micrograph, hematoxylin and eosin<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>SD</b> striated duct (faint basal striations)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>b</b> scanning electron micrograph, duct cells seen from the side<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>L</b> lumen<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>B</b> basal ends with interlocking membrane folds<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>c</b> scanning electron micrograph of the cell bases (basal lamina removed)</div></figure>\n<p>On a slide, a striated duct lies among the acini and is wider than they are: a ring of tall pink cells with round central nuclei around an open lumen. A duct that lies in a band of connective tissue between lobules is an interlobular duct (1.3).</p>\n<h4>Why striated ducts look striated</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The basal cell membrane folds deeply inward and interlocks with the neighboring cells. The folds greatly enlarge the surface that carries the ion pumps.</li>\n<li>Long mitochondria line up between the folds and supply the energy to pump Na+. Under the light microscope, these rows look like stripes in the base of the cell.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>The result: hypotonic saliva</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The ducts remove more salt than they add, and their walls let little water through.</li>\n<li>So the final saliva is <b>hypotonic</b> (less salty than plasma) and rich in K+ and HCO3−.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Plasma cells and IgA</h4>\n<p>Plasma cells in the connective tissue around the ducts and acini make IgA. Acinar cells add the secretory component, which carries IgA into the saliva.</p>","s":"Salivary glands","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Ducts between lobules, and the whole path","p":"<h4>The whole path of saliva</h4>\n<p>Acinus → intercalated duct → striated duct → <b>interlobular duct</b> → lobar duct → main (excretory) duct → mouth.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G020-12.jpg\" alt=\"Duct system of a salivary gland\"><figcaption>Duct system of a salivary gland <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 12</small></figcaption></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Intralobular</b> ducts (intercalated and striated) lie inside the lobule, among the acini.</li>\n<li><b>Interlobular</b> ducts lie in the connective tissue <b>septa between lobules</b>. Their lining changes as they get larger: simple cuboidal → simple columnar → stratified cuboidal or columnar, or pseudostratified columnar.</li>\n<li>The main duct becomes nonkeratinized stratified squamous near its opening into the mouth.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Naming a duct on a slide</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>First look at what surrounds it. A duct surrounded by acini is intralobular. A duct sitting in a band of pink connective tissue between lobules is interlobular.</li>\n<li>Then look at the lining: low cuboidal and small (intercalated), tall pink with basal stripes (striated), or larger and often layered (interlobular).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Parts of a secretory unit to be able to label</h4>\n<p>Serous acinus, mucous tubule, serous demilune (1.4), myoepithelial cells, intercellular secretory canaliculi, intercalated duct, striated duct.</p>","s":"Salivary glands","sn":1,"pn":"1.3"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Three glands, three kinds of saliva","p":"<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Gland</th><th>Secretory units</th><th>Notes</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Parotid</b></td><td><b>Exclusively serous</b></td><td>The largest. Makes most of the salivary amylase. Many fat cells; long intercalated and striated ducts.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Submandibular</b></td><td><b>Mixed, mostly serous</b></td><td>Mostly serous acini, with some mucous tubules capped by serous demilunes. Its serous cells add lysozyme.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Sublingual</b></td><td><b>Mixed, mostly mucous</b></td><td>Mostly mucous tubules; the serous cells are mainly in demilunes.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Minor glands (lips, cheeks, palate)</td><td>Mostly mucous</td><td>Exception: the von Ebner glands of the tongue are serous.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G020-19.jpg\" alt=\"Serous demilunes: mucous acini capped with serous cells\"><figcaption>Serous demilunes: mucous acini capped with serous cells <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 19</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>A</b> serous acinus<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>S</b> serous demilunes (arrows; dark caps on mucous tubules)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>M</b> mucous cells (pale)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>ID</b> small intralobular duct (arrow)</div></figure>\n<h4>Serous demilunes</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A crescent (half-moon) of dark serous cells capping the end of a pale mucous tubule. The serous cells add the protein part of the mixed secretion through tiny canaliculi between the mucous cells.</li>\n<li>They occur only in the <b>mixed glands</b>: the sublingual and the submandibular. The lecture says they are <b>mostly seen in the sublingual</b>, and also in the submandibular.</li>\n<li>Junqueira adds that the crescent shape is partly an artifact of fixation; in living tissue the serous cells line the tubule.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What saliva contains</h4>\n<p>Water, mucins, amylase (mostly from the parotid), lysozyme (submandibular), IgA with its secretory component, and ions (more K+ and HCO3−, less Na+ than plasma).</p>\n<h4>Clinical</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Xerostomia: dry mouth (for example Sjögren syndrome, an autoimmune attack on salivary glands; radiation; drugs).</li>\n<li>Sialorrhea: too much saliva (drooling).</li>\n<li>Mumps: a viral infection that swells the parotid glands.</li>\n<li>Sialolithiasis: stones in the ducts, most often in the submandibular gland.</li>\n</ul>","s":"Salivary glands","sn":1,"pn":"1.4"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Recognizing the parotid","p":"<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G020-15.jpg\" alt=\"Parotid gland: serous acini, intercalated and striated ducts, fat cells\"><figcaption>Parotid gland: serous acini, intercalated and striated ducts, fat cells <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 15</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>a</b> parotid, plastic section, toluidine blue-based stain<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>SD</b> striated duct<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>A</b> serous acini<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>ID</b> intercalated duct<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>b</b> parotid, hematoxylin and eosin<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>CT</b> connective-tissue septum<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>lines at left edge of the atlas plate (labels cut off), top to bottom</b> venule; interlobular excretory duct; fat cells; interlobular excretory duct; serous acini; connective-tissue septa between lobules; myoepithelial cells (on inset I)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>I</b> serous acinus<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>II</b> intercalated duct<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>III</b> striated duct<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>IV</b> interlobular excretory duct</div></figure>\n<h4>Checklist</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Every secretory unit is a dark, granular <b>serous acinus</b> with round nuclei. There are no pale mucous cells anywhere.</li>\n<li>Many <b>fat cells</b> (empty white circles) among the acini; they increase with age.</li>\n<li>Many intercalated and <b>striated ducts</b>. The pink striated ducts stand out among the purple acini.</li>\n<li>Septa of connective tissue carry the interlobular ducts.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Look-alikes</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Pancreas: also all serous, but it has pale islets, pale centroacinar cells inside its acini, no striated ducts and few fat cells (2.3).</li>\n<li>Submandibular gland: mostly serous too, but some pale mucous tubules and demilunes show up (1.6).</li>\n<li>Liver and spleen have no acini at all.</li>\n</ul>","s":"Salivary glands","sn":1,"pn":"1.5"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Recognizing the submandibular and sublingual, and all three together","p":"<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G020-17.jpg\" alt=\"Submandibular gland: mostly serous acini, some mucous acini with demilunes, many striated ducts\"><figcaption>Submandibular gland: mostly serous acini, some mucous acini with demilunes, many striated ducts <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 17</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Submandibular gland</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Mostly <b>dark serous acini</b>, with scattered <b>pale mucous tubules</b>. Many of the mucous tubules are capped by serous demilunes.</li>\n<li>Many striated ducts; lobules separated by septa.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G020-73.jpg\" alt=\"Sublingual gland: mostly pale mucous acini\"><figcaption>Sublingual gland: mostly pale mucous acini <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 73</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Sublingual gland</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Mostly <b>pale, bluish-gray mucous tubules</b> with flattened nuclei at the base. Even at low power the whole field looks pale.</li>\n<li>Serous cells appear mainly as demilunes.</li>\n<li>Few striated ducts. Skeletal muscle of the floor of the mouth may sit next to it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Quick key for all three</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Gland</th><th>Dark serous units</th><th>Pale mucous units</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Parotid</td><td>All</td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Submandibular</td><td>Most</td><td>Some, with demilunes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sublingual</td><td>Few (mostly demilunes)</td><td>Most</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The exocrine pancreas is all serous, like the parotid. So any pale mucous unit or demilune in the field rules out both of them (2.3).</p>","s":"Salivary glands","sn":1,"pn":"1.6"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Two glands in one: acini and islets","p":"<p>The pancreas is a <b>mixed gland</b>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Exocrine part</b> (most of the gland): serous acini that make digestive enzymes, and ducts that carry them, with a bicarbonate-rich fluid, to the duodenum.</li>\n<li><b>Endocrine part</b>: the <b>islets of Langerhans</b>, small clusters of hormone-making cells scattered among the acini.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G020-24.jpg\" alt=\"Pancreas: serous acini, ducts, and pale islets\"><figcaption>Pancreas: serous acini, ducts, and pale islets <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 24</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Organization</h4>\n<p>A thin capsule; septa divide it into lobules. Very little connective tissue lies between the acini: only reticular fibers and capillaries.</p>\n<h4>Islets of Langerhans</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Round clusters of <b>pale-staining</b> cells arranged in cords, threaded with many capillaries and wrapped in a fine capsule of reticular fibers.</li>\n<li><b>No ducts</b>: the hormones go straight into the blood.</li>\n<li>Main cells: beta cells (insulin; the most numerous), alpha cells (glucagon), delta cells (somatostatin). They look alike on H&amp;E; special stains tell them apart.</li>\n<li>Job: control blood glucose.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>On a slide</h4>\n<p>A field of tightly packed, dark serous acini with pale round islands scattered through it: pancreas. No salivary gland has islets.</p>","s":"Pancreas","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Acinar cells and centroacinar cells","p":"<h4>Acinar (zymogenic) cells</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Pyramidal serous cells arranged around a <b>small central lumen</b>.</li>\n<li>Base: round nucleus and a lot of rough endoplasmic reticulum, so it is <b>basophilic</b> (purple). The Golgi lies above the nucleus.</li>\n<li>Top (apex): many <b>zymogen granules</b> (eosinophilic, pink) holding inactive enzymes. They are released into the lumen.</li>\n<li>This polarity, a broad base to make protein and a narrow top to release it, sends the enzymes into the lumen and on into the ducts.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G020-26.jpg\" alt=\"Pancreatic acinus: zymogen granules, centroacinar cells, intercalated duct\"><figcaption>Pancreatic acinus: zymogen granules, centroacinar cells, intercalated duct <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 26</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>A</b> pancreatic acini<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>F</b> fibroblast (arrow)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>arrow without a letter</b> centroacinar cell (pale nucleus inside the acinus)</div></figure>\n<h4>Centroacinar cells</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Pale, flat cells with pale nuclei <b>in the center of the acinus</b>.</li>\n<li>They are the first cells of the intercalated duct, which begins inside the acinus. So they are continuous with the intercalated duct cells.</li>\n<li>They have <b>no zymogen granules</b>. Their pale staining is normal.</li>\n<li>They are found <b>only in the pancreas</b>, not in the parotid or any other salivary gland.</li>\n<li>With the intercalated duct cells, they secrete a bicarbonate-rich fluid when stimulated by secretin (2.4).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Duct path</h4>\n<p>Centroacinar cells → intercalated ducts (simple squamous to low cuboidal) → intralobular ducts → interlobular ducts (columnar) → main pancreatic duct → duodenum, joining the common bile duct at the major duodenal papilla. There are <b>no striated ducts</b>.</p>","s":"Pancreas","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Pancreas or parotid?","p":"<p>The exocrine pancreas and the parotid are both made of dark serous acini full of zymogen granules. Look for these four features to tell them apart (lecture slide 30):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Pancreas</th><th>Serous salivary gland (parotid)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Islets of Langerhans</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Centroacinar cells</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Myoepithelial cells</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Striated ducts</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Extra clues: the parotid has many fat cells; the pancreas has few.</li>\n<li>In the pancreas the intercalated duct starts inside the acinus (as centroacinar cells). In salivary glands it starts outside the acinus.</li>\n<li>When no islet is in the field, look at the ducts. In the pancreas the ducts among the acini are lined by pale cells. A wide duct of tall, bright pink cells (a striated duct) means a salivary gland.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G020-77.jpg\" alt=\"Pancreas at low power, with islets\"><figcaption>Pancreas at low power, with islets <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 77</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Pancreas","sn":2,"pn":"2.3"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Blood supply and the portal triad","p":"<h4>Overview</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The largest gland in the body. It is covered by <b>Glisson capsule</b>, connective tissue that enters at the hilum with the vessels.</li>\n<li>Built from: hepatic lobules; plates of liver cells (hepatocytes); sinusoids between the plates; a fine framework of reticular fibers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Two blood supplies</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Vessel</th><th>What it brings</th><th>Share of liver blood flow</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Portal vein</b></td><td>Blood from the gut and spleen: rich in absorbed nutrients (and toxins), <b>poor in oxygen</b></td><td>About 75% (the anatomy lecture says 70%)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Hepatic artery</b></td><td><b>Oxygen-rich</b> arterial blood</td><td>About 25%</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Both vessels branch together and empty into the sinusoids, where the two kinds of blood <b>mix</b>. So every hepatocyte gets both.</li>\n<li>Blood then leaves through the central vein → hepatic veins → inferior vena cava.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G020-45.jpg\" alt=\"Portal triad: portal venule, hepatic arteriole, interlobular bile ductule\"><figcaption>Portal triad: portal venule, hepatic arteriole, interlobular bile ductule <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 45</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Portal triad (portal area)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>At the corners of each lobule, in a little connective tissue, three tubes run together:\n<ul>\n<li>a branch of the <b>portal vein</b> (portal venule);</li>\n<li>a branch of the <b>hepatic artery</b> (hepatic arteriole);</li>\n<li>a <b>bile ductule</b> (interlobular bile duct), lined by cuboidal epithelium.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>Lymphatic vessels and nerves run there too.</li>\n<li>On a slide: the portal venule is the <b>largest tube, with a thin wall</b>; it is the one that brings the oxygen-poor, nutrient-rich blood. The hepatic arteriole is small and round with a thick muscular wall. The bile ductule is a ring of cuboidal cells and carries bile out (3.2).</li>\n</ul>","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"The classic lobule on a slide","p":"<h4>The classic hepatic lobule</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A hexagon with a <b>central vein</b> in the middle and <b>portal triads at the corners</b>.</li>\n<li>Plates of hepatocytes, one or two cells thick, radiate from the central vein toward the corners like spokes. Sinusoids run between the plates.</li>\n<li>In humans, very little connective tissue separates neighboring lobules, so their borders are hard to see. Imagine lines joining the portal areas. In the pig, thick septa outline every lobule.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G020-43.jpg\" alt=\"Liver lobules: central veins and portal triads\"><figcaption>Liver lobules: central veins and portal triads <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 43</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>lines at left edge (labels cut off), top to bottom</b> bile ductule, portal venule, hepatic arteriole (one portal triad); connective-tissue septum between lobules; sinusoids; central vein; plates of hepatocytes<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>bracket under the picture</b> one hepatic lobule</div></figure>\n<h4>Limiting plate</h4>\n<p>The ring of hepatocytes directly around a portal area. It forms a smooth, even border between the portal connective tissue and the lobule.</p>\n<h4>Telling the tubes apart</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Structure</th><th>Look</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Portal venule</td><td>The largest lumen; thin wall; often an irregular shape</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hepatic arteriole</td><td>Small, round; thick wall of smooth muscle; small lumen</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bile ductule</td><td>A ring of cuboidal epithelial cells around a small lumen; no muscle</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Central vein</td><td>Alone in the middle of the lobule, with no artery or duct beside it; very thin wall; sinusoids open straight into it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sinusoids</td><td>Pale channels between the hepatocyte plates</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Special stains of the liver</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Silver (reticulin) stain: black reticular fibers outline the plates and sinusoids.</li>\n<li>India ink injection: picked up by Kupffer cells (3.5).</li>\n<li>Periodic acid–Schiff (PAS): glycogen in hepatocytes (3.3).</li>\n</ul>","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Hepatocytes","p":"<h4>Structure</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Large polygonal cells with a round central nucleus; often <b>binucleated</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Stable cells</b>: they rarely divide, but they can start dividing again to regrow the liver after injury or partial removal.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G020-48.jpg\" alt=\"Hepatocyte surfaces: sinusoidal, intercellular and canalicular\"><figcaption>Hepatocyte surfaces: sinusoidal, intercellular and canalicular <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 48</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Three surfaces (a polarized cell)</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Surface</th><th>Faces</th><th>Features</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sinusoidal (basal or basolateral domain)</td><td>The space of Disse and the sinusoid</td><td>Many microvilli. Takes up material from the blood; releases plasma proteins and glucose.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Intercellular (lateral)</td><td>Neighboring hepatocytes</td><td>Cell junctions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Canalicular (apical domain)</td><td>The bile canaliculus, a tiny groove between two hepatocytes</td><td>Sealed by tight junctions; secretes bile</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>So the hepatocyte is polarized even though it lines no duct: bile leaves from the apical (canalicular) side, and plasma proteins leave from the basolateral (sinusoidal) side.</p>\n<h4>Organelles and their jobs</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Organelle</th><th>Job</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Rough endoplasmic reticulum (with Golgi)</td><td>Makes <b>plasma proteins</b>: albumin, clotting factors, lipoproteins</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Smooth endoplasmic reticulum</td><td><b>Detoxification</b> and drug metabolism; bile acid synthesis; conjugation of bilirubin</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Glycogen granules</td><td>Stored glucose, taken up from the blood in the space of Disse. Stains magenta with periodic acid–Schiff (<b>PAS</b>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mitochondria, lysosomes, peroxisomes</td><td>Energy, breakdown of old material, fatty acid metabolism</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Functions of the liver (lecture list)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Forms bile.</li>\n<li>Stores glycogen and buffers blood glucose.</li>\n<li>Makes urea from ammonia (the kidneys then excrete it).</li>\n<li>Metabolizes cholesterol and fat.</li>\n<li>Endocrine secretion: plasma proteins, insulin-like growth factor (IGF).</li>\n<li>Detoxification.</li>\n<li>Processes several steroid hormones and vitamin D.</li>\n</ul>","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Sinusoids and the space of Disse","p":"<h4>Sinusoids</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Wide, irregular capillaries between the plates of hepatocytes. Blood flows through them from the portal triads to the central vein.</li>\n<li>On a slide: the <b>pale channels between the plates</b>; they hold red blood cells.</li>\n<li>Their lining is <b>fenestrated endothelium</b> with large open pores and gaps between the cells, and <b>no continuous basal lamina</b> (a discontinuous capillary). An ordinary fenestrated capillary (kidney, gut, endocrine glands) is narrower, and its pores sit on a continuous basal lamina.</li>\n<li>Kupffer cells sit in the lining, facing the lumen (3.5).</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G020-53.jpg\" alt=\"Space of Disse between the sinusoid endothelium and the hepatocyte\"><figcaption>Space of Disse between the sinusoid endothelium and the hepatocyte <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 53</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>sER</b> smooth endoplasmic reticulum<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>rER</b> rough endoplasmic reticulum<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>red circle</b> hepatic artery branch<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>green outline</b> portal vein branch<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>cyan circle</b> bile duct<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>blue double line</b> limiting plate (&#x27;mura&#x27;: ring of hepatocytes around the portal canal)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>11</b> slide number of the source set, not a structure</div></figure>\n<h4>Space of Disse (perisinusoidal space)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A thin space between the sinusoid endothelium and the hepatocytes.</li>\n<li>Hepatocyte microvilli reach into it. Plasma passes freely into it through the endothelial pores.</li>\n<li>It holds reticular fibers and the <b>Ito cells</b> (hepatic stellate cells, 3.6).</li>\n<li>Job: <b>exchange of materials between hepatocytes and the blood</b>. With no solid barrier, contact is as close as possible.</li>\n<li>On an electron micrograph: the lumen is bounded by a very thin endothelial cell with pores. Just outside it lies the space of Disse, packed with hepatocyte microvilli. The bile canaliculus also holds microvilli, but it lies between two hepatocytes, away from the sinusoid (3.7).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Space of Mall (periportal space)</h4>\n<p>A space between the portal connective tissue and the limiting plate. Fluid from the space of Disse drains into it, and it is where liver lymph begins.</p>\n<h4>Where each cell lives</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Cell</th><th>Location</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Endothelial cell</td><td>Lines the sinusoid</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Kupffer cell</td><td>Inside the sinusoid, part of its lining</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ito cell</td><td>In the space of Disse, outside the endothelium</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>The interstitium, in general</h4>\n<p>The interstitium is everything between the cells outside the vessels: ground substance (glycosaminoglycans and proteoglycans), interstitial fluid, fibers (collagen, reticular, elastic), and the fibroblasts that make them.</p>","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.4"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Kupffer cells","p":"<ul>\n<li>The resident <b>macrophages</b> of the liver. They are star-shaped with long processes (stellate macrophages). They come from blood monocytes.</li>\n<li>Location: <b>within the sinusoids</b>, as part of the lining, facing the lumen.</li>\n<li>Jobs:\n<ul>\n<li>phagocytose aged red blood cells, bacteria and debris arriving from the gut;</li>\n<li>act as <b>antigen-presenting cells</b>.</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G020-51.jpg\" alt=\"Kupffer cells\"><figcaption>Kupffer cells <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 51</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>K</b> Kupffer cells (black from injected India ink)</div></figure>\n<h4>On a slide</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>On routine H&amp;E: a cell lying in the sinusoid, between the hepatocytes, whose cytoplasm holds engulfed brown or yellow particles.</li>\n<li>After an injection of India ink (or carbon particles), Kupffer cells eat the particles and show up as black cells along the sinusoids.</li>\n<li>On an electron micrograph: a large cell inside the sinusoid lumen, against the endothelium, with phagocytic vacuoles.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Names not to mix up</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Name</th><th>What it is</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Kupffer cell</td><td>Liver macrophage, in the sinusoid</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ito (hepatic stellate) cell</td><td>Stores fat and vitamin A, in the space of Disse (3.6)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Langerhans cell</td><td>Antigen-presenting cell of the skin's epidermis, not the liver</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Islet of Langerhans</td><td>Endocrine cluster of the pancreas</td></tr>\n</table></div>","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.5"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Ito cells, fatty liver and cirrhosis","p":"<h4>Ito cells (hepatic stellate cells, perisinusoidal lipocytes)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Live in the <b>space of Disse</b>.</li>\n<li>Store fat droplets and <b>vitamin A</b>, the body's main vitamin A store. (The slide says they secrete vitamin A; they store it and release it as needed.)</li>\n<li>After injury (alcohol, fat, viruses), they activate into myofibroblast-like cells and <b>secrete collagen and other matrix</b>. This scar tissue is fibrosis.</li>\n<li>On a slide: a cell beside a sinusoid that holds a <b>large, clear, round fat droplet</b>, with its small dark nucleus pushed to the edge.</li>\n<li>Names from other organs: stave cells are the long endothelial cells that line the venous sinuses of the spleen; epithelioreticular cells form the framework of the thymus. Neither is found in the liver.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G020-52.jpg\" alt=\"Ito cell with lipid droplets in the space of Disse\"><figcaption>Ito cell with lipid droplets in the space of Disse <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 52</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>HS</b> hepatic stellate (Ito) cell with lipid droplets<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>K</b> Kupffer cells<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>PS</b> perisinusoidal space of Disse (arrow)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>E</b> endothelial cell<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>H</b> hepatocytes<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>S</b> sinusoid</div></figure>\n<h4>Fatty liver disease</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Fat droplets build up inside hepatocytes (steatosis). Linked to obesity, type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome and alcohol.</li>\n<li>Nonalcoholic fatty liver (NAFL) means fat only. Nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) means fat plus inflammation and hepatocyte injury, which can lead to fibrosis.</li>\n<li>Why a fatty liver matters: NASH carries a higher risk of <b>advanced fibrosis, cirrhosis and liver cancer</b>. Fat by itself does not prove that any scar is present.</li>\n<li>Ultrasound shows fat (a brighter, echogenic liver) but cannot tell NAFL from NASH. <b>Liver biopsy</b> is the gold standard.</li>\n<li>A trichrome stain colors collagen blue. It shows the scar laid down by activated Ito cells in the space of Disse, first around the central veins.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Cirrhosis</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The end stage: bands of fibrous scar surround nodules of regrowing hepatocytes, and the normal lobule pattern is lost.</li>\n<li>Fewer working hepatocytes means low albumin (swelling, fluid in the belly) and fewer clotting factors (easy bruising and bleeding). Blocked flow raises portal pressure (varices).</li>\n<li>Main causes: long-term heavy alcohol use, fatty liver disease, chronic viral hepatitis.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Regeneration</h4>\n<p>Hepatocytes are stable cells and can regrow lost liver. After severe injury, progenitor cells in the canals of Hering help (3.7).</p>","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.6"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Bile flow","p":"<p>Inside the lobule, blood and bile flow in <b>opposite directions</b>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blood: portal triad (edge) → sinusoids → <b>central vein</b> (center).</li>\n<li>Bile: hepatocytes → bile canaliculi → toward the portal triad (edge).</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G020-47.jpg\" alt=\"Flow of blood (toward the central vein) and bile (toward the portal area)\"><figcaption>Flow of blood (toward the central vein) and bile (toward the portal area) <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 47</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>arrows</b> flow: blood (blue venous, red arterial) along the sinusoid into the central vein; bile (green) the other way, to the bile ductule</div></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Passage</th><th>Wall</th><th>Where</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Bile canaliculus</b></td><td>No lining cells of its own. It is a tiny groove between two hepatocytes, sealed by tight junctions; hepatocyte microvilli project into it.</td><td>Inside the plates</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Canal of Hering</b> (intralobular bile ductule)</td><td>Lined partly by hepatocytes and partly by cuboidal <b>cholangiocytes</b>. Holds progenitor cells that help the liver <b>regenerate</b> after injury.</td><td>At the edge of the lobule</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Interlobular bile ductule</b></td><td>Cuboidal to columnar cholangiocytes</td><td>In the portal triad</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>On a slide, a bile canaliculus is a thin line or tiny channel between the facing sides of two hepatocytes, inside the plate, and it branches. A sinusoid is much wider, lies between the plates and holds blood cells.</p>\n<p>Then bile flows to the right and left hepatic ducts → common hepatic duct → cystic duct to the gallbladder for storage, or common bile duct → duodenum.</p>","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.7"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Three ways to draw a lobule, and the zones","p":"<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G020-56.jpg\" alt=\"Classic lobule, portal lobule and portal acinus\"><figcaption>Classic lobule, portal lobule and portal acinus <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 56</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Model</th><th>Shape and center</th><th>What it emphasizes</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Classic lobule</b></td><td>Hexagon; central vein in the center, portal triads at the corners</td><td>Anatomy; blood draining to the central vein</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Portal lobule</b></td><td>Triangle; a portal triad in the center, three central veins at the corners</td><td>Bile secretion: all its bile drains to the bile duct of the central triad</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Portal acinus</b></td><td>Diamond between two central veins; its short axis runs between two portal triads, along the vessels that feed it</td><td>Blood supply and metabolism: the oxygen and nutrient gradient</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Zones of the portal acinus</h4>\n<p>Blood enters at the portal triad and flows toward the central vein. It loses oxygen and nutrients along the way, so the hepatocytes form three zones.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G020-59.jpg\" alt=\"Zones 1, 2 and 3 of the portal acinus\"><figcaption>Zones 1, 2 and 3 of the portal acinus <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 59</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Zone</th><th>Position</th><th>Oxygen</th><th>Main work</th><th>Most affected by</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Zone 1</b> (periportal)</td><td>Nearest the portal triad</td><td><b>Most</b></td><td>Oxidative metabolism; first to meet incoming blood</td><td><b>Viral hepatitis</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Zone 2 (intermediate)</td><td>Between</td><td>Medium</td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Zone 3</b> (centrilobular)</td><td>Nearest the central vein</td><td><b>Least</b></td><td>Drug and ammonia detoxification (the most drug-metabolizing enzymes)</td><td><b>Hypoxia</b> and ischemic necrosis (shock, heart failure); fat build-up; <b>acetaminophen</b> and <b>alcohol</b> damage</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Board review books add that a few toxins that need no activation (such as cocaine) hit zone 1 first, because it meets them first. The 2026 lecture ties drug, acetaminophen and alcohol injury to zone 3.</p>","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.8"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"The gallbladder","p":"<h4>Job</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Stores bile and <b>concentrates</b> it (up to about 10 times) by absorbing water and salt.</li>\n<li>Contracts and empties bile into the duodenum in response to <b>cholecystokinin (CCK)</b> from duodenal enteroendocrine cells, after a fatty meal.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G020-65.jpg\" alt=\"Gallbladder wall: folded mucosa, muscular layer, adventitia\"><figcaption>Gallbladder wall: folded mucosa, muscular layer, adventitia <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 65</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>LP</b> lamina propria<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>(a) arrows</b> simple columnar epithelium<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>M</b> muscularis (bracket line)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>A</b> adventitia (bracket line)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>G</b> mucus secretory granules<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>MV</b> microvilli<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>(b) arrows and arrowheads</b> spaces between folded lateral cell membranes (water leaves the cells here)</div></figure>\n<h4>The wall has only three layers</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Mucosa</b>:\n<ul>\n<li><b>Simple columnar epithelium</b> with apical microvilli, many mitochondria and lateral infoldings (folded side membranes). These are the features of a cell built to absorb water.</li>\n<li>Lamina propria.</li>\n<li>Highly folded when the gallbladder is empty. The branching folds look like villi, but they are not true villi.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><b>Muscular layer</b> (muscularis): thin, randomly arranged bundles of smooth muscle with elastic fibers.</li>\n<li><b>Adventitia</b>: thick connective tissue with blood vessels, lymphatics and nerves, where the gallbladder is attached to the liver. Serosa covers the free surface.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The gallbladder has <b>no muscularis mucosae and no submucosa</b>. It has no glands except a few mucous glands near the neck.</p>\n<h4>Clinical</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Gallstones (cholelithiasis): cholesterol stones (most common) or pigment stones (from breakdown of red cells, for example in sickle cell anemia). Pain in the right upper quadrant after fatty meals.</li>\n<li>Cholecystitis: inflammation, usually from a stone blocking the cystic duct. Right upper quadrant pain spreading to the right shoulder, nausea, fever.</li>\n<li>In cystic fibrosis, thick secretions can block the pancreatic and bile ducts, causing fatty, foul-smelling stools.</li>\n</ul>","s":"Gallbladder","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"}]};